adians)\nprint(theta_degrees)\n```\n```output\n74.06565131265105\n```\nThe angle between the two lines is approximately \\(74.07^\\circ\\).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{74.07}\n\\]", "To find the distance between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) in a Cartesian plane, we use the distance formula:\n\\[ \\text{distance} = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2} \\]\n\nFor the points \\((2, -6)\\) and \\((-4, 3)\\), we can substitute \\(x_1 = 2\\), \\(y_1 = -6\\), \\(x_2 = -4\\), and \\(y_2 = 3\\) into the formula:\n\\[ \\text{distance} = \\sqrt{((-4) - 2)^2 + (3 - (-6))^2} = \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + (3 + 6)^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 81} = \\sqrt{117} = \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 13} = 3\\sqrt{13} \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Coordinates of the points\nx1, y1 = 2, -6\nx2, y2 = -4, 3\n\n# Calculate the distance using the distance formula\ndistance = math.sqrt((x2 - x1)**2 + (y2 - y1)**2)\nprint(distance)\n```\n```output\n10.816653826391969\n```\nThe exact value of the distance is \\(3\\sqrt{13}\\), which is approximately 10.816653826391969 when evaluated numerically. Therefore, the distance between the points \\((2, -6)\\) and \\((-4, 3)\\) in simplest radical form is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3\\sqrt{13}}\n\\]", "To find the distance between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) in a Cartesian plane, we use the distance formula:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nGiven the points \\((2, -6)\\) and \\((-4, 3)\\), we can substitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{((-4) - 2)^2 + (3 - (-6))^2}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the expression inside the square root:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + (3 + 6)^2} = \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + 9^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 81} = \\sqrt{117} = \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 13} = 3\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nSo, the distance between the points \\((2, -6)\\) and \\((-4, 3)\\) is \\(3\\sqrt{13}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this by running the calculation in Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Coordinates of the poin