he amplitude \\( a \\) is half the distance between the maximum and minimum values of the function.\n   - The vertical shift \\( d \\) is the average of the maximum and minimum values of the function.\n\n3. **Determine the Period:**\n   - The period \\( T \\) of the sine function is the distance over which the function repeats itself.\n   - For the standard sine function \\( \\sin(x) \\), the period is \\( 2\\pi \\). For \\( \\sin(bx) \\), the period is \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{b} \\).\n\n4. **Determine the Phase Shift:**\n   - The phase shift is the horizontal shift of the function. It can be determined by looking at the x-intercept or the starting point of the sine wave.\n\nLet's analyze the given graph:\n\n- The maximum value is 3 and the minimum value is -1.\n- The amplitude \\( a \\) is \\(\\frac{3 - (-1)}{2} = 2\\).\n- The vertical shift \\( d \\) is \\(\\frac{3 + (-1)}{2} = 1\\).\n\n- The period of the function appears to be \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\), so \\( b = 3 \\).\n\n- The phase shift can be determined by looking at the starting point of the sine wave. The graph starts at \\( x = -\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\) (where the sine function normally starts at \\( x = 0 \\)). This means the phase shift is \\( -\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\).\n\nSo, the function can be written as \\( y = 2 \\sin(3x + c) + 1 \\). To find \\( c \\), we use the phase shift:\n\n\\[ 3 \\left( -\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right) + c = 0 \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{3\\pi}{2} + c = 0 \\]\n\\[ c = \\frac{3\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nHowever, we need the smallest possible positive value of \\( c \\). Since the sine function is periodic with period \\( 2\\pi \\), we can reduce \\( \\frac{3\\pi}{2} \\) by subtracting \\( 2\\pi \\):\n\n\\[ c = \\frac{3\\pi}{2} - 2\\pi = -\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nSince we need the smallest positive value, we add \\( 2\\pi \\) to \\( -\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\):\n\n\\[ c = -\\frac{\\pi}{2} + 2\\pi = \\frac{3\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nBut we can reduce it further by subtracting \\( 2\\pi \\):\n\n\\[ c = \\frac{3\\pi}{2} - 2\\pi = -\\frac{\\p