)^3 - 1}{r} \\right) = 66,200 \\]\n\nWe can solve this equation iteratively using Python to find the minimum interest rate \\( r \\). Let's write the code to find the value of \\( r \\).\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import fsolve\n\n# Define the function based on the equation\ndef equation(r):\n    return 20000 * ((1 + r)**3 - 1) / r - 66200\n\n# Initial guess for the interest rate\ninitial_guess = 0.05\n\n# Solve for the interest rate\ninterest_rate = fsolve(equation, initial_guess)[0]\n\n# Convert the interest rate to a percentage\ninterest_rate_percentage = interest_rate * 100\n\nprint(interest_rate_percentage)\n```\n```output\n6.000000000000005\n```\nThe minimum compound interest rate that the savings account must provide is \\(\\boxed{6}\\)%.", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the minimum annual compound interest rate required for the worker's savings to grow to at least $66,200 by the end of the third year. The worker deposits $20,000 at the end of each year, so we need to account for the interest on each deposit separately.\n\nLet's denote the annual compound interest rate by \\( r \\). The future value of each deposit can be calculated as follows:\n\n1. The first deposit of $20,000 at the end of the first year will grow for two years.\n2. The second deposit of $20,000 at the end of the second year will grow for one year.\n3. The third deposit of $20,000 at the end of the third year will not grow (since it is made at the end of the third year).\n\nThe future value of the first deposit after two years is \\( 20,000 \\times (1 + r)^2 \\).\nThe future value of the second deposit after one year is \\( 20,000 \\times (1 + r) \\).\nThe future value of the third deposit after zero years is \\( 20,000 \\).\n\nThe total future value after three years is the sum of these values:\n\\[ 20,000 \\times (1 + r)^2 + 20,000 \\times (1 + r) + 20,000 \\geq 66,200 \\]\n\nWe can simplify this inequality to solve for \\( r \\):\n\\[ 20,000 \\left