s problem, we need to determine the minimum annual compound interest rate that will allow the worker to have at least $66,200 in his savings account by the end of the third year, given that he deposits $20,000 at the end of each year.\n\nLet's break down the problem step by step:\n\n1. **Define the Variables:**\n   - Let \\( r \\) be the annual interest rate (as a decimal).\n   - The worker deposits $20,000 at the end of each year.\n\n2. **Calculate the Future Value of Each Deposit:**\n   - The first deposit of $20,000 will compound for 2 years.\n   - The second deposit of $20,000 will compound for 1 year.\n   - The third deposit of $20,000 will not compound at all.\n\n3. **Formulate the Equation:**\n   - The future value of the first deposit after 2 years is \\( 20,000 \\cdot (1 + r)^2 \\).\n   - The future value of the second deposit after 1 year is \\( 20,000 \\cdot (1 + r) \\).\n   - The future value of the third deposit after 0 years is \\( 20,000 \\).\n   - The total future value after 3 years must be at least $66,200.\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[ 20,000 \\cdot (1 + r)^2 + 20,000 \\cdot (1 + r) + 20,000 \\geq 66,200 \\]\n\n4. **Solve the Equation:**\n   - We can simplify the equation to:\n     \\[ 20,000 \\cdot ((1 + r)^2 + (1 + r) + 1) \\geq 66,200 \\]\n   - Dividing both sides by 20,000, we get:\n     \\[ (1 + r)^2 + (1 + r) + 1 \\geq 3.31 \\]\n   - Let \\( x = 1 + r \\). Then the equation becomes:\n     \\[ x^2 + x + 1 \\geq 3.31 \\]\n   - Rearranging terms, we get:\n     \\[ x^2 + x - 2.31 \\geq 0 \\]\n\nWe can solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 1 \\), and \\( c = -2.31 \\).\n\nLet's solve this using Python:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the quadratic equation\nequation = x**2 + x - 2.31\n\n# Solve the quadratic equation\nsolutions = sp.solve(equation, x)\nprint(solutions)\n```\n```output\n