ts\nn = sp.lcm([sp.denom(sp.arg(root).as_numer_denom()[1]) for root in roots])\nprint(n)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nIt seems there was an error in the code. The output of 1 is incorrect because it doesn't consider the correct interpretation of the roots being \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity. Let's correct the approach.\n\nWe know the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( e^{\\pi i / 3} \\), \\( e^{-\\pi i / 3} \\), \\( e^{5\\pi i / 3} \\), and \\( e^{7\\pi i / 3} \\). These roots are the 12th roots of unity because they can be expressed as \\( e^{2\\pi i k / 12} \\) for \\( k = 1, 5, 7, 11 \\).\n\nTo confirm this, we can use the fact that the roots of unity are periodic with period \\( n \\). The smallest \\( n \\) such that all these roots are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity is the least common multiple (LCM) of the denominators of the arguments of the roots when expressed in the form \\( e^{2\\pi i k / n} \\).\n\nHere's the corrected code:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nz = sp.symbols('z')\n\n# Define the polynomial\npolynomial = z**4 + z**2 + 1\n\n# Find the roots of the polynomial\nroots = sp.solve(polynomial, z)\n\n# Extract the arguments of the roots\narguments = [sp.arg(root) for root in roots]\n\n# Convert arguments to a common form and find the denominators\ndenominators = [sp.denom(sp.arg(root).as_numer_denom()[1]) for root in roots]\n\n# Find the least common multiple of the denominators\nn = sp.lcm(denominators)\nprint(n)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nIt seems there is still an issue with the code. The output of 1 is incorrect because it doesn't correctly interpret the roots as \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity. Let's manually verify the roots and their order.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are:\n\\[ z = e^{\\pi i / 3}, \\quad z = e^{-\\pi i / 3}, \\quad z = e^{5\\pi i / 3}, \\quad z = e^{7\\pi i / 3}. \\]\nThese roots are the 12th roots of unity because they can be expres