en convert it back to sine functions.\n\nLet's start by expressing \\((\\sin x)^7\\) in terms of complex exponentials:\n\\[\n(\\sin x)^7 = \\left(\\frac{e^{ix} - e^{-ix}}{2i}\\right)^7.\n\\]\nExpanding this using the binomial theorem, we get:\n\\[\n(\\sin x)^7 = \\frac{1}{(2i)^7} \\sum_{k=0}^{7} \\binom{7}{k} (e^{ix})^{7-k} (e^{-ix})^k (-1)^k.\n\\]\nSimplifying the expression, we have:\n\\[\n(\\sin x)^7 = \\frac{1}{128i} \\sum_{k=0}^{7} \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^k e^{i(7-2k)x}.\n\\]\nWe need to collect the terms that correspond to \\(\\sin 7x\\), \\(\\sin 5x\\), \\(\\sin 3x\\), and \\(\\sin x\\). These terms will be the ones where the exponent of \\(e^{ix}\\) is odd and the coefficient of \\(x\\) is 7, 5, 3, or 1.\n\nLet's extract the coefficients for \\(\\sin 7x\\), \\(\\sin 5x\\), \\(\\sin 3x\\), and \\(\\sin x\\):\n\\[\n\\sin 7x \\text{ term: } \\frac{1}{128i} \\left( \\binom{7}{0} (-1)^0 e^{i7x} + \\binom{7}{3} (-1)^3 e^{-ix} + \\binom{7}{4} (-1)^4 e^{ix} + \\binom{7}{6} (-1)^6 e^{-3ix} + \\binom{7}{7} (-1)^7 e^{-5ix} \\right).\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin 5x \\text{ term: } \\frac{1}{128i} \\left( \\binom{7}{1} (-1)^1 e^{3ix} + \\binom{7}{2} (-1)^2 e^{-ix} + \\binom{7}{5} (-1)^5 e^{-ix} + \\binom{7}{6} (-1)^6 e^{-3ix} \\right).\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin 3x \\text{ term: } \\frac{1}{128i} \\left( \\binom{7}{1} (-1)^1 e^{5ix} + \\binom{7}{2} (-1)^2 e^{ix} + \\binom{7}{4} (-1)^4 e^{-ix} + \\binom{7}{5} (-1)^5 e^{-3ix} \\right).\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin x \\text{ term: } \\frac{1}{128i} \\left( \\binom{7}{1} (-1)^1 e^{5ix} + \\binom{7}{2} (-1)^2 e^{3ix} + \\binom{7}{3} (-1)^3 e^{ix} + \\binom{7}{4} (-1)^4 e^{-ix} + \\binom{7}{5} (-1)^5 e^{-3ix} + \\binom{7}{6} (-1)^6 e^{-5ix} + \\binom{7}{7} (-1)^7 e^{-7ix} \\right).\n\\]\nWe can now extract the coefficients for \\(\\sin 7x\\), \\(\\sin 5x\\), \\(\\sin 3x\\), and \\(\\sin x\\):\n\\[\na = \\frac{1}{128i} \\left( \\binom{7}{0} (-1)^0 + \\binom{7}{4} (-1)^4 + \\binom{7}{6} (-1)^6 \\right) = \\frac{1}{128i} (1 + 35 + 7) = \\frac{43}{128i}.\n\\]\n\\[\nb =