: \\(5x - 1 = -(x + 3)\\)\n\\[5x - 1 = -x - 3\\]\n\\[5x + x = -3 + 1\\]\n\\[6x = -2\\]\n\\[x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\]\n\nWe have two potential solutions: \\(x = 1\\) and \\(x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\). To determine which is the largest, we compare the two values.\n\nClearly, \\(1 > -\\frac{1}{3}\\), so the largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation is \\(x = 1\\).\n\nLet's verify this solution using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the two potential solutions\nx1 = 1\nx2 = -1/3\n\n# Verify the solutions by substituting back into the original equation\nsolution1 = abs(5 * x1 - 1) == x1 + 3\nsolution2 = abs(5 * x2 - 1) == x2 + 3\n\n# Determine the largest solution\nlargest_solution = max(x1, x2) if solution1 and solution2 else None\nprint(largest_solution)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nThe largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation \\(|5x-1|=x+3\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).", "To solve the equation \\(|5x - 1| = x + 3\\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The expression \\(|5x - 1|\\) can be broken into two cases:\n\n1. \\(5x - 1 = x + 3\\)\n2. \\(-(5x - 1) = x + 3\\)\n\nWe will solve each case separately and then determine which solution is the largest.\n\n### Case 1: \\(5x - 1 = x + 3\\)\n\n\\[\n5x - 1 = x + 3\n\\]\n\\[\n5x - x = 3 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n4x = 4\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 1\n\\]\n\n### Case 2: \\(-(5x - 1) = x + 3\\)\n\n\\[\n-5x + 1 = x + 3\n\\]\n\\[\n-5x - x = 3 - 1\n\\]\n\\[\n-6x = 2\n\\]\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nWe have two potential solutions: \\(x = 1\\) and \\(x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\). To determine which is the largest, we compare the two values. Clearly, \\(1 > -\\frac{1}{3}\\).\n\nSo, the largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation \\(|5x - 1| = x + 3\\) is \\(x = 1\\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\n# Define the function to check the solutions\r\ndef check_solution(x):\r\n    return abs(5 * x - 1) == x + 3\r\n\r\n# Potential solutions\r\nsolutions = [1, -1/3]\r\n\r\n# Verify the 