\n\n\\[\n5x - 1 = x + 3\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\( x \\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n4x - 1 = 3\n\\]\n\nAdd 1 to both sides:\n\n\\[\n4x = 4\n\\]\n\nDivide by 4:\n\n\\[\nx = 1\n\\]\n\n### Case 2: \\( -(5x - 1) = x + 3 \\)\n\n\\[\n-5x + 1 = x + 3\n\\]\n\nAdd \\( 5x \\) to both sides:\n\n\\[\n1 = 6x + 3\n\\]\n\nSubtract 3 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n-2 = 6x\n\\]\n\nDivide by 6:\n\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nWe now have two potential solutions: \\( x = 1 \\) and \\( x = -\\frac{1}{3} \\). We need to check which of these solutions satisfy the original equation \\( |5x - 1| = x + 3 \\).\n\n1. For \\( x = 1 \\):\n\n\\[\n|5(1) - 1| = 1 + 3 \\implies |4| = 4 \\implies 4 = 4\n\\]\n\nThis is true, so \\( x = 1 \\) is a valid solution.\n\n2. For \\( x = -\\frac{1}{3} \\):\n\n\\[\n|5\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) - 1| = -\\frac{1}{3} + 3 \\implies \\left| -\\frac{5}{3} - 1 \\right| = \\frac{8}{3} \\implies \\left| -\\frac{8}{3} \\right| = \\frac{8}{3} \\implies \\frac{8}{3} = \\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis is true, so \\( x = -\\frac{1}{3} \\) is also a valid solution.\n\nSince we are asked to find the largest value of \\( x \\) that satisfies the equation, the answer is \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the function to check the solutions\r\ndef check_solution(x):\r\n    return abs(5 * x - 1) == x + 3\r\n\r\n# Potential solutions\r\nsolutions = [1, -1/3]\r\n\r\n# Check which solutions are valid\r\nvalid_solutions = [x for x in solutions if check_solution(x)]\r\n\r\n# Find the largest valid solution\r\nlargest_solution = max(valid_solutions)\r\nprint(largest_solution)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nThe largest value of \\( x \\) that satisfies the equation \\( |5x - 1| = x + 3 \\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).", "To solve the equation \\( |5x - 1| = x + 3 \\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The expression inside the absolute value can be either positive or negative, leading to two separate cases:\n\n1. \\( 5x - 1 = x + 3 \\)\n2. \\