he inequality\na = sp.symbols('a')\ninequality = a**3 - a**2 - 2*a <= 0\n\n# Solve the inequality\nsolution = sp.solve(inequality, a)\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n((2 <= a) & (a < oo)) | ((-oo < a) & (a <= -1))\n```\nThe solution to the inequality \\(a^3 - a^2 - 2a \\leq 0\\) is \\((-\\infty < a \\leq -1) \\cup (2 \\leq a < \\infty)\\). However, since \\(a\\) must be non-negative (as it is a coefficient in the functions \\(ax^2\\) and \\(ax + 2a\\)), we only consider the interval \\(0 \\leq a \\leq 2\\).\n\nTherefore, the largest value of \\(a\\) such that the graph of \\(y = f(x)\\) intersects every horizontal line at least once is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 3339, 2961, and 1491, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm is an efficient method for computing the greatest common divisor of two integers. To find the GCD of more than two integers, we can iteratively apply the Euclidean algorithm.\n\nThe steps are as follows:\n\n1. Compute the GCD of the first two numbers (3339 and 2961).\n2. Use the result from step 1 to compute the GCD with the third number (1491).\n\nLet's start by computing the GCD of 3339 and 2961 using the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm states that the GCD of two numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) (where \\(a > b\\)) is the same as the GCD of \\(b\\) and \\(a \\mod b\\). We repeat this process until \\(b\\) becomes 0. The last non-zero remainder is the GCD.\n\nFirst, we compute the GCD of 3339 and 2961:\n\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(3339, 2961) = \\text{GCD}(2961, 3339 \\mod 2961) = \\text{GCD}(2961, 378)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(2961, 378) = \\text{GCD}(378, 2961 \\mod 378) = \\text{GCD}(378, 225)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(378, 225) = \\text{GCD}(225, 378 \\mod 225) = \\text{GCD}(225, 153)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(225, 153) = \\text{GCD}(153, 225 \\mod 153) = \\text{GCD}(153, 72)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(153, 72) = \\text{GCD}(72, 153 \\mod 72) = \\text{GCD}(72, 9)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(72, 9) = 