stances\r\ndistances = [sqrt((point[0] - x)**2 + (point[1] - y)**2) for x, y in points]\r\n\r\n# Sum the distances\r\ntotal_distance = sum(distances)\r\nprint(total_distance.simplify())\n```\n```output\n4*sqrt(4*sqrt(41) + 36)\n```\nThe sum of the distances from the four points of intersection to the point \\((-3, 2)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4\\sqrt{4\\sqrt{41} + 36}}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to find the points of intersection of the two given curves and then calculate the sum of the distances from these points to the point \\((-3, 2)\\).\n\nThe equations of the curves are:\n1. \\(x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0\\)\n2. \\(x^2 - y^2 + 6x + 16y - 46 = 0\\)\n\nFirst, we will find the points of intersection of these two curves. We can subtract the second equation from the first to eliminate the \\(x^2\\) and \\(6x\\) terms:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72) - (x^2 - y^2 + 6x + 16y - 46) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n2y^2 - 40y + 118 = 0\n\\]\n\nDividing through by 2, we get:\n\\[\ny^2 - 20y + 59 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this quadratic equation for \\(y\\) using the quadratic formula \\(y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -20\\), and \\(c = 59\\):\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{20 \\pm \\sqrt{400 - 236}}{2} = \\frac{20 \\pm \\sqrt{164}}{2} = \\frac{20 \\pm 2\\sqrt{41}}{2} = 10 \\pm \\sqrt{41}\n\\]\n\nSo the solutions for \\(y\\) are \\(y = 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\) and \\(y = 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\).\n\nNext, we substitute these \\(y\\)-values back into one of the original equations to find the corresponding \\(x\\)-values. Let's use the first equation \\(x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0\\):\n\nFor \\(y = 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\):\n\\[\nx^2 + (10 + \\sqrt{41})^2 + 6x - 24(10 + \\sqrt{41}) + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\nx^2 + (100 + 20\\sqrt{41} + 41) + 6x - 240 - 24\\sqrt{41} + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x + 131 - 4\\sqrt{41} = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(x\\). We can solve it using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}