ueError):\n        return float(\'inf\')\n\n# Generate all possible ways to place parentheses\ndef generate_parentheses(expr):\n    n = len(expr)\n    # We need to consider all possible positions for parentheses\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        left = generate_parentheses(expr[:i])\n        right = generate_parentheses(expr[i:])\n        for l in left:\n            for r in right:\n                yield f"({l}){expr[i]}{r}"\n    # Base case: if the expression has no parentheses, just return it\n    yield expr\n\n# Generate all possible expressions with parentheses\nall_expressions = set()\nfor i in range(1, len(expression)):\n    for left in generate_parentheses(expression[:i]):\n        for right in generate_parentheses(expression[i:]):\n            all_expressions.add(f"({left}){expression[i]}{right}")\n\n# Evaluate all possible expressions and find the minimum value\nmin_value = float(\'inf\')\nfor expr in all_expressions:\n    value = evaluate_expression(expr)\n    if value < min_value:\n        min_value = value\n\nprint(min_value)\n```\n```output\nRecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded while calling a Python object\n```\nThe recursion error indicates that the current approach of generating all possible expressions with parentheses is too computationally intensive and causes a stack overflow. Instead, we can use a more direct approach by evaluating all possible combinations of parentheses placements manually.\n\nWe can use a simpler method by considering all possible ways to split the expression into sub-expressions and then evaluating each combination. We can use a loop to try different placements of parentheses and evaluate the resulting expressions.\n\nHere\'s the revised approach:\n\n1. Split the expression into different possible sub-expressions.\n2. Evaluate each possible combination of sub-expressions.\n3. Find the minimum value among all possible evaluations.\n\nLet\'s write the code to achieve this:\n\n```python\nimport itertools\n\n# The 