etermine its behavior under the transformation $x \\to -x$.\n\nLet's break it down step-by-step:\n\n1. Start with the innermost function $f(x)$. Since $f$ is odd, $f(-x) = -f(x)$.\n2. Next, consider $g(f(x))$. Since $g$ is even, $g(f(-x)) = g(-f(x)) = g(f(x))$ (because $g$ is even and $f$ is odd).\n3. Now consider $f(g(f(x)))$. Since $f$ is odd, $f(g(f(-x))) = f(g(f(x)))$ (because $g(f(x))$ is even).\n4. Next, consider $g(f(g(f(x))))$. Since $g$ is even, $g(f(g(f(-x)))) = g(f(g(f(x))))$ (because $f(g(f(x)))$ is even).\n5. Finally, consider $f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))$. Since $f$ is odd, $f(f(g(f(g(f(-x))))) = -f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))$.\n\nThis shows that $f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))$ satisfies the property of an odd function, i.e., $f(f(g(f(g(f(-x))))) = -f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))$.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))$ is odd.\n\nLet's confirm this with a simple Python code using sympy to symbolically verify the properties.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the symbols\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the functions f and g as symbolic functions\nf = sp.Function('f')\ng = sp.Function('g')\n\n# Define the nested function\nnested_function = f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))\n\n# Check if the function is odd\nis_odd = sp.simplify(nested_function.subs(x, -x) + nested_function) == 0\nprint(is_odd)\n```\n```output\nFalse\n```\nThe output `False` indicates that the symbolic verification using sympy did not confirm that the nested function $f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))$ is odd. This suggests that the symbolic verification might not have fully captured the properties of the functions $f$ and $g$ as odd and even, respectively. Let's manually recheck the reasoning step-by-step.\n\n1. Start with the innermost function $f(x)$. Since $f$ is odd, $f(-x) = -f(x)$.\n2. Next, consider $g(f(x))$. Since $g$ is even, $g(f(-x)) = g(-f(x)) = g(f(x))$ (because $g$ is even and $f$ is odd).\n3. Now consider $f(g(f(x)))$. Since $f$ is odd, $f(g(f(-x))) = f(g(f(x)))$ (because $g(f(x))$ is even).\n4. Next, consider $g(f