ction\ncomposite_function = f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))\n\n# Substitute -x into the composite function\ncomposite_function_neg_x = composite_function.subs(x, -x)\n\n# Simplify the expression under the assumption that f is odd and g is even\ncomposite_function_neg_x_simplified = sp.simplify(composite_function_neg_x.subs({f(-x): -f(x), g(-x): g(x)}))\n\n# Check if the function is odd\nis_odd = sp.simplify(composite_function_neg_x_simplified + composite_function) == 0\n\nprint(is_odd)\n```\n```output\nFalse\n```\nThe output `False` indicates that the function $f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))$ is not odd. This suggests that our initial reasoning might have overlooked some details. Let's re-evaluate the behavior of the composite function step-by-step.\n\n1. **Behavior of $f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))$:**\n   - Since $f(x)$ is odd, $f(-x) = -f(x)$.\n   - Since $g(x)$ is even, $g(-x) = g(x)$.\n\n2. **Substitute $-x$ into the Composite Function:**\n   Let's carefully analyze the behavior of the composite function $f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))$ when $x$ is replaced by $-x$:\n   \\[\n   f(f(g(f(g(f(-x)))))) = f(f(g(f(g(-f(x)))))) = f(f(g(f(-g(f(x)))))) = f(f(g(-f(g(f(x)))))) = f(-f(g(f(g(f(x)))))) = -f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))\n   \\]\n   Here, the last step follows because $f(g(f(g(f(x)))))$ is the result of an odd function ($f$) composed with even and odd functions, and the composition of an odd function with any number of even and odd functions results in an odd function. Therefore, $f(g(f(g(f(x)))))$ is odd, and applying the odd function $f$ to it results in an odd function.\n\nThus, $f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))$ is indeed an odd function.\n\nLet's confirm this reasoning with a more detailed symbolic verification using sympy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the symbols\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the odd and even function properties\nf = sp.Function('f')\ng = sp.Function('g')\n\n# Define the composite function\ncomposite_function = f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))\n\n# Substitute -x into the composite function\ncomposi