\geq \\sqrt[n]{x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_n}.\n   \\]\n   Applying AM-GM to the expression \\(\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{c}{a} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b + c} \\cdot \\frac{b}{c}} = 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{b}{b + c}}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\frac{b}{b + c} < 1\\), we have \\(3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{b}{b + c}} < 3\\). However, this doesn\'t directly give us the lower bound. We need to find a more precise lower bound.\n\n3. **Finding the Lower Bound:**\n   To find the lower bound, we can use the fact that \\(\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 2\\) for positive real numbers \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\). This can be shown by considering the expression in different cases or using calculus.\n\n4. **Finding the Upper Bound:**\n   The upper bound is not bounded, as we can make the expression arbitrarily large by choosing \\(a\\) and \\(c\\) to be very small and \\(b\\) to be very large.\n\nLet\'s implement this in Python using sympy to verify our findings.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\na, b, c = sp.symbols(\'a b c\', positive=True)\n\n# Define the expression\nexpression = c/a + a/(b + c) + b/c\n\n# Simplify the expression\nsimplified_expression = sp.simplify(expression)\n\n# Find the critical points by taking the partial derivatives and setting them to zero\npartial_a = sp.diff(simplified_expression, a)\npartial_b = sp.diff(simplified_expression, b)\npartial_c = sp.diff(simplified_expression, c)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\ncritical_points = sp.solve([partial_a, partial_b, partial_c], (a, b, c), dict=True)\n\n# Evaluate the expression at the critical points\ncritical_values = [simplified_expression.subs(point) for point in critical_points]\n\n# Check the behavior of the expression as a, b, c approach certain limits\nlimit_a_small = sp.limit(simplified_expression, a, 0, dir=\'+\')\nlimit_c_small = sp.limit(simplified_expression, c, 0