et's calculate \\(|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)|\\):\n\n1. \\(a + b = 1 - 1 = 0\\) (This is zero, so we need to consider another configuration or check if there's a different way to maximize the expression.)\n2. \\(a + c = 1 + i\\)\n3. \\(a + d = 1 - i\\)\n4. \\(b + c = -1 + i\\)\n5. \\(b + d = -1 - i\\)\n6. \\(c + d = i - i = 0\\) (This is zero, so we need to consider another configuration or check if there's a different way to maximize the expression.)\n\nSince both configurations we tried resulted in some terms being zero, let's consider a different approach. We can use the fact that the product of the distances from any point on the unit circle to the other three points of a rectangle inscribed in the unit circle is a constant. Specifically, if \\(a, b, c, d\\) are the vertices of a rectangle, then the product of the distances from any vertex to the other three vertices is \\(4\\).\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of \\(|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)|\\) is \\(4^3 = 64\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the complex numbers a, b, c, d\na, b, c, d = sp.symbols('a b c d', complex=True)\n\n# Define the constraints\nconstraints = [sp.Abs(a) - 1, sp.Abs(b) - 1, sp.Abs(c) - 1, sp.Abs(d) - 1, a + b + c + d]\n\n# Define the expression to maximize\nexpression = sp.Abs((a + b) * (a + c) * (a + d) * (b + c) * (b + d) * (c + d))\n\n# Substitute a = 1, b = -1, c = i, d = -i\na_val = 1\nb_val = -1\nc_val = sp.I\nd_val = -sp.I\n\n# Evaluate the expression\nexpression_value = expression.subs({a: a_val, b: b_val, c: c_val, d: d_val})\n\n# Simplify the expression\nexpression_value = sp.simplify(expression_value)\n\nprint(expression_value)\n```\n```output\n0\n```\nIt seems that the configuration \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -1\\), \\(c = i\\), and \\(d = -i\\) resulted in the expression evaluating to zero, which is not the maximum value we are looking for. Let's consider another configuration that respects 