( x = 50 \\):\n\\[ y = 60 - 50 = 10 \\]\n\\[ 5(50) - 2(10) = 250 - 20 = 230 \\neq 232 \\]\n\nIf \\( x = 52 \\):\n\\[ y = 60 - 52 = 8 \\]\n\\[ 5(52) - 2(8) = 260 - 16 = 244 \\neq 232 \\]\n\nIf \\( x = 51 \\):\n\\[ y = 60 - 51 = 9 \\]\n\\[ 5(51) - 2(9) = 255 - 18 = 237 \\neq 232 \\]\n\nIt seems we need to check the integer solution more carefully. Let's use Python to solve the equations accurately.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variables\nx, y = symbols('x y')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = Eq(x + y, 60)\neq2 = Eq(5*x - 2*y, 232)\n\n# Solve the equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (x, y))\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n{x: 352/7, y: 128/7}\n```\nThe solution to the system of equations is \\( x = \\frac{352}{7} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{128}{7} \\). Since \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) must be integers, we need to find the closest integer values that satisfy both equations.\n\nLet's check the integer solutions around \\(\\frac{352}{7} \\approx 50.29\\) and \\(\\frac{128}{7} \\approx 18.29\\). We need to check if \\( x = 50 \\) or \\( x = 51 \\) (since 352 is not exactly divisible by 7).\n\nIf \\( x = 50 \\):\n\\[ y = 60 - 50 = 10 \\]\n\\[ 5(50) - 2(10) = 250 - 20 = 230 \\neq 232 \\]\n\nIf \\( x = 51 \\):\n\\[ y = 60 - 51 = 9 \\]\n\\[ 5(51) - 2(9) = 255 - 18 = 237 \\neq 232 \\]\n\nIf \\( x = 52 \\):\n\\[ y = 60 - 52 = 8 \\]\n\\[ 5(52) - 2(8) = 260 - 16 = 244 \\neq 232 \\]\n\nIt seems we need to check if there is an integer solution that satisfies both equations. Let's use Python to check for the integer solutions more accurately.\n```python\n# Check integer solutions around the non-integer solution\npossible_solutions = []\n\n# Check for integer solutions\nfor x_val in range(50, 53):\n    y_val = 60 - x_val\n    if 5 * x_val - 2 * y_val == 232:\n        possible_solutions.append((x_val, y_val))\n\nprint(possible_solutions)\n```\n```output\n[]\n```\nIt appears that there are no integer solutions in the range we checked. Let's re-evaluate 