fferent ways to partition the number 5 into up to 2 parts, where the order of the parts does not matter.\n\nThe possible partitions of 5 into up to 2 parts are:\n1. All 5 balls in one box and 0 in the other: (5, 0)\n2. 4 balls in one box and 1 in the other: (4, 1)\n3. 3 balls in one box and 2 in the other: (3, 2)\n\nSince the boxes are indistinguishable, the partitions (5, 0), (4, 1), and (3, 2) are the only distinct ways to distribute the balls. Therefore, there are 3 distinct ways to distribute the balls.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# The function to calculate the number of ways to distribute n indistinguishable balls into k indistinguishable boxes\ndef count_ways(n, k):\n    if k == 1:\n        return 1  # Only one way to put all balls in one box\n    if k == n:\n        return 1  # Only one way to put one ball in each box\n    if k > n:\n        return 0  # Not possible to distribute if k > n\n    return count_ways(n, k-1) + count_ways(n-k, k)\n\n# Number of balls\nn = 5\n# Number of boxes\nk = 2\n\n# Calculate the number of ways\nways = count_ways(n, k)\nprint(ways)\n```\n```output\nRecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded in comparison\n```\nThe recursion error indicates that the function is recursing too deeply. This can happen because the function is not designed to handle the specific case of distributing indistinguishable balls into indistinguishable boxes efficiently.\n\nInstead of using recursion, we can directly enumerate the possible distributions and count the unique ones. Here is the revised approach:\n\n1. We need to find all possible ways to partition the number 5 into up to 2 parts.\n2. Since the boxes are indistinguishable, we only need to consider the unique partitions.\n\nLet's write the code to enumerate these partitions and count the unique ones.\n```python\n# Function to find all unique partitions of n into up to k parts\ndef find_partitions(n, k):\n    if k == 1:\n        return [(n,)]\n    if k == n:\n        return [(1,)*n]\n    partitions = []\n    for i in range(n + 1):\n        if i <= n:\n            for p in find_partitions(n - i, k - 1):\n                partitions.append((i,) + p)\n    return partitions\n\n# Number of balls\nn = 5\n# Number of boxes\nk = 2\n\n# Find all partitions of n into up to k parts\npartitions = find_partitions(n, k)\n\n# Remove duplicate partitions (since boxes are indistinguishable)\nunique_partitions = set(tuple(sorted(p)) for p in partitions)\n\n# The number of unique partitions is the number of ways to distribute the balls\nways = len(unique_partitions)\nprint(ways)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe number of ways to put 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "When dealing with indistinguishable balls and indistinguishable boxes, the problem reduces to finding the number of ways to partition the integer 5 (the number of balls) into up to 2 parts (the number of boxes). This is a classic combinatorial problem.\n\nThe partitions of 5 into up to 2 parts are:\n1. 5 + 0\n2. 4 + 1\n3. 3 + 2\n\nSo, there are exactly 3 ways to distribute 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes.\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code. We can write a function to generate all possible partitions of a number `n` into up to `k` parts and then count the number of such partitions.\n\nHere is the Python code to solve this problem:\n```python\ndef count_partitions(n, k):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n    if n < 0 or k == 0:\n        return 0\n    return count_partitions(n, k-1) + count_partitions(n-k, k)\n\n# Number of balls\nn = 5\n# Number of boxes\nk = 2\n\n# Calculate the number of ways to partition n balls into up to k boxes\nnum_ways = count_partitions(n, k)\nprint(num_ways)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe number of ways to put 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of ways to distribute 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes. This is a combinatorial problem that can be approached by considering the different possible distributions of the balls.\n\nSince the boxes are indistinguishable, the order of the boxes does not matter. The possible distributions of the balls are as follows:\n\n1. All 5 balls in one box and 0 in the other.\n2. 4 balls in one box and 1 in the other.\n3. 3 balls in one box and 2 in the other.\n4. 2 balls in one box and 3 in the other (this is the same as the previous case since the boxes are indistinguishable).\n5. 1 ball in one box and 4 in the other (this is the same as the second case).\n6. 0 balls in one box and 5 in the other (this is the same as the first case).\n\nSo, we only need to count the distinct distributions, which are:\n\n1. (5, 0)\n2. (4, 1)\n3. (3, 2)\n\nThis gives us 3 distinct ways to distribute the balls. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# The number of ways to distribute 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes\n# is the number of distinct partitions of 5 into up to 2 parts.\n\n# We can list out the partitions:\n# (5, 0), (4, 1), (3, 2)\n\n# Since the boxes are indistinguishable, we don't need to consider permutations of the same partition.\n# So, the number of distinct ways is simply the number of partitions listed above.\n\n# Let's count the partitions programmatically to ensure accuracy.\ndef count_partitions(n, k):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n    if n < 0 or k == 0:\n        return 0\n    return count_partitions(n, k-1) + count_partitions(n-k, k)\n\n# For 5 balls and 2 boxes\nways = count_partitions(5, 2)\nprint(ways)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe number of ways to distribute 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To solve the problem of putting 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes, we need to consider the different possible distributions of the balls. Since the boxes are indistinguishable, the order of the box