mbols('B Y')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = sp.Eq(3*B, 4*Y)\neq2 = sp.Eq(3*(B + 5), 7*(Y - 3))\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = sp.solve((eq1, eq2), (B, Y))\nblue_marbles_initial = solution[B]\n\n# Print the initial number of blue marbles\nprint(blue_marbles_initial)\n```\n```output\n16\n```\nThe initial number of blue marbles in the bag is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).", "Let's denote the number of blue marbles initially in the bag as \\( B \\) and the number of yellow marbles initially in the bag as \\( Y \\). According to the problem, the initial ratio of blue to yellow marbles is 4:3, so we can write the equation:\n\n\\[ \\frac{B}{Y} = \\frac{4}{3} \\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[ 3B = 4Y \\]\n\nAfter adding 5 blue marbles and removing 3 yellow marbles, the new ratio of blue to yellow marbles is 7:3. So, we can write the equation:\n\n\\[ \\frac{B + 5}{Y - 3} = \\frac{7}{3} \\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[ 3(B + 5) = 7(Y - 3) \\]\n\nSimplifying the second equation, we get:\n\n\\[ 3B + 15 = 7Y - 21 \\]\n\nWe can now solve these two equations simultaneously. First, we'll express \\( Y \\) in terms of \\( B \\) from the first equation:\n\n\\[ Y = \\frac{3B}{4} \\]\n\nNext, we'll substitute \\( Y \\) in the second equation:\n\n\\[ 3B + 15 = 7\\left(\\frac{3B}{4}\\right) - 21 \\]\n\nSimplifying the right side, we get:\n\n\\[ 3B + 15 = \\frac{21B}{4} - 21 \\]\n\nTo eliminate the fraction, we'll multiply every term by 4:\n\n\\[ 12B + 60 = 21B - 84 \\]\n\nRearranging terms to isolate \\( B \\):\n\n\\[ 60 + 84 = 21B - 12B \\]\n\n\\[ 144 = 9B \\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[ B = \\frac{144}{9} = 16 \\]\n\nNow we can find \\( Y \\):\n\n\\[ Y = \\frac{3B}{4} = \\frac{3 \\times 16}{4} = 12 \\]\n\nSo, the initial number of blue marbles in the bag is \\( B = 16 \\). We can verify this by checking the conditions of the problem:\n\n1. Initially, the ratio of blue to yellow marbles is \\( \\frac{16}{12} = \\frac{4}{3} \\), which is correct.\n2. After adding 5 blue marbles and 