-\\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the angle $\\theta$ is:\n\\[\n\\theta = \\cos^{-1}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = 120^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{120}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the calculations are accurate.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\r\n\r\n# Define points A, B, and C\r\nA = np.array([1, -11, 2])\r\nB = np.array([3, -4, 1])\r\nC = np.array([-2, 1, -1])\r\n\r\n# Calculate vectors BA and BC\r\nBA = A - B\r\nBC = C - B\r\n\r\n# Calculate the dot product of BA and BC\r\ndot_product = np.dot(BA, BC)\r\n\r\n# Calculate the magnitudes of BA and BC\r\nmagnitude_BA = np.linalg.norm(BA)\r\nmagnitude_BC = np.linalg.norm(BC)\r\n\r\n# Calculate the cosine of the angle\r\ncos_theta = dot_product / (magnitude_BA * magnitude_BC)\r\n\r\n# Calculate the angle in degrees\r\nangle_degrees = np.degrees(np.arccos(cos_theta))\r\nprint(angle_degrees)\n```", "To find the angle $\\angle ABC$, we need to calculate the angle between the vectors $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ and $\\overrightarrow{BC}$. The formula to find the angle $\\theta$ between two vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\theta = \\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\|\\mathbf{u}\\| \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|}\n\\]\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ is the dot product of the vectors, and $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|$ and $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ are the magnitudes of the vectors.\n\nFirst, we need to find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ and $\\overrightarrow{BC}$:\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{BA} = A - B = (1 - 3, -11 - (-4), 2 - 1) = (-2, -7, 1)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{BC} = C - B = (-2 - 3, 1 - (-4), -1 - 1) = (-5, 5, -2)\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the dot product of $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ and $\\overrightarrow{BC}$:\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{BA} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{BC} = (-2)(-5) + (-7)(5) + (1)(-2) = 10 - 35 - 2 = -27\n\\]\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitudes of $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ and $\\overrightarrow{BC}$:\n\n\\[\n\\|\\overrightarrow{BA}\\| = \