ac{\\pi}{2}\\) for all \\(x \\neq 0\\).\n2. \\(\\tan^{-1}(x) + \\cot^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) for all \\(x \\neq 0\\).\n\nThis means that \\(\\sin \\left( \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\right) = \\sin \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = 1\\).\n\nHowever, the problem states that \\(\\sin \\left( \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\\), which creates a contradiction because \\(\\sin \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = 1 \\neq \\frac{1}{3}\\).\n\nTherefore, there are no solutions to the equation \\(\\sin \\left( \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\\).\n\nThe final answer is: \\(\\boxed{\\text{No solution}}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this reasoning with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\r\n\r\n# Define the function to check the equation\r\ndef check_equation(x):\r\n    angle = np.arctan(x) + np.arctan(1/x)\r\n    return np.sin(angle)\r\n\r\n# Test the function with a range of x values\r\nx_values = np.linspace(-100, 100, 1000)\r\nresults = [check_equation(x) for x in x_values if x != 0]\r\n\r\n# Check if any of the results are equal to 1/3\r\nsolutions = [x for x, result in zip(x_values, results) if np.isclose(result, 1/3, atol=1e-6)]\r\n\r\nprint(solutions)\n```", "To solve the given problem, we need to find all solutions to the equation:\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nFirst, we should recall the relationship between the inverse tangent and inverse cotangent functions:\n\\[\n\\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = \\tan^{-1} (x).\n\\]\nThis is true for \\(x > 0\\). For \\(x < 0\\), the relationship is slightly different, but the sum \\(\\tan^{-1}(x) + \\cot^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\) will still simplify to \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) due to the symmetry of the functions.\n\nTherefore, the equation simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( \\tan^