ion property of the cross product, we get:\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 3 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) + 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\nWe know from the problem statement that:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSubstituting these values in, we get:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNow we can perform the scalar multiplication and vector addition:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nAdding these vectors together, we get:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]', "To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product to break this down:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = 2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} + 2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\n\nSince the cross product is anti-commutative (\\(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} = -\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{u}\\)) and the cross product of a vector with itself is the zero vector (\\(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{0}\\)), we can simplify the expression:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) + 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - \\mathbf{0}.\n\\]\n\nWe know the values of \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\), \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values into the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = 6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow we can compute the result by performing the scalar multiplications and vector additions:\n\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nAdding these vectors together:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given vectors\na_cross_b = np.array([6, -7, 3])\na_cross_c = np.array([4, 7, 2])\nb_cross_c = np.array([1, -7, 18])\n\n# Compute b_cross_a\nb_cross_a = -a_cross_b\n\n# Compute the expression (2b - a) x (3c + a)\nresult = 6 * b_cross_c + 2 * b_cross_a - 3 * a_cross_c\nprint(result)\n```", 'To solve the problem, we need to compute the vector cross product $(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})$. We can use the properties of the cross product to simplify this expression. The cross product is distributive over vector addition and scalar multiplication, so we can expand the expression as follows:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of the cross product, we can simplify this to:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) + 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\n\nSince the cross product of any vector with itself is the zero vector, $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}$, the expression simplifies further to:\n\n\\[\n6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) + 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\n\nWe are given:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSince $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})$, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values into the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow we can compute this step by step:\n\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nAdding these together, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]', 'To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product over vector addition and subtraction, and the scalar multiplication property of the cross product.\n\nThe cross product distributes over addition and subtraction, and scalar multiplication can be factored out. Therefore, we can expand the expression as follows:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{a} \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) - \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\nSince the cross product of any vector with itself is the zero vector, \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}\\). Also, we can factor out the scalars:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}).\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\mathbf{a} \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) = -3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\nSo the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) + 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\nWe know from the problem statement that:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSubstituting these values into the expression, we get:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNow we can compute the scalar multiplication and then add/subtract the resulting vectors:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nAdding these vectors together, we get:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]', 'To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product over vector addition and the properties of the cross product to simplify the expression.\n\nThe cross product distributes over vector addition, so we have:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} + (2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\nUsing the distributive property again, we get:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} = 2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\nAnd,\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = 2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\nSince the cross product of a vector with itself is zero, \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}\\), we have:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}).\n\\]\nWe know that \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = - (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})\\), so:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = 2 (- (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})) = -2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}).\n\\]\nPutting it all together, we have:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}).\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = 6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = -3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}) = -2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nAdding these vectors together, we get:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 - 21 + 14 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]', 'To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product over vector addition and subtraction, and the properties of the cross product with scalar multiplication.\n\nThe distributive property of the cross product over vector addition states that \\((\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) \\times \\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{u} \\times (\\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{w}) = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w}\\). The property of the cross product with scalar multiplication states that \\((k \\mathbf{u}) \\times \\mathbf{v} = k (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) = \\mathbf{u} \\times (k \\mathbf{v})\\).\n\nUsing these properties, we can expand the expression as follows:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\nWe can simplify each term:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}),\n\\]\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) = -2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}),\n\\]\n\\[\n(-\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) = -3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}),\n\\]\n\\[\n(-\\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.\n\\]\nSo the expression becomes:\n\\[\n6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} - 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNow we can compute the scalar multiplication and vector subtraction:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nAdding these vectors together:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]', "To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product $(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})$. We can use the distributive property of the cross product over vector addition and the properties of the cross product to simplify the expression.\n\nThe distributive property of the cross product over vector addition is given by:\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) \\times \\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\times (\\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{w}) = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w}.\n\\]\n\nUsing these properties, we can expand the expression as follows:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}).\n\\]\n\nSince the cross product of a vector with itself is zero, the term $-\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}$ is zero. Also, we can use the scalar multiplication property of the cross product, which states that $k(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) = (k\\mathbf{u}) \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{u} \\times (k\\mathbf{v})$. Thus, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) + 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\n\nWe know the values of $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}$, $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}$, and $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}) = -\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values into the expression, we get:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow we can compute the scalar multiplication and vector addition:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad -3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nAdding these vectors together, we get:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given vectors\na_cross_b = np.array([6, -7, 3])\na_cross_c = np.array([4, 7, 2])\nb_cross_c = np.array([1, -7, 18])\n\n# Calculate the required cross product\nresult = 6 * b_cross_c + 2 * (-a_cross_b) - 3 * a_cross_c\nprint(result)\n```", 'To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product over vector addition and the scalar multiplication property.\n\nThe distributive property of the cross product is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\times (\\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{w}) = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) \\times \\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}.\n\\]\nAlso, the scalar multiplication property is:\n\\[\n(c \\mathbf{u}) \\times \\mathbf{v} = c (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) = \\mathbf{u} \\times (c \\mathbf{v}).\n\\]\n\nUsing these properties, we can expand the expression \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\) as follows:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} + (2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\nThis can be further expanded to:\n\\[\n= 2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} + 2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}\\), the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n= 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}) + 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}).\n\\]\nWe know that \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = - (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})\\), so we can substitute the known values:\n\\[\n= 6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\left( - \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\right).\n\\]\nNow we can perform the scalar multiplications and vector additions/subtractions:\n\\[\n= \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nCombining these vectors, we get:\n\\[\n= \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 - 21 + 14 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]', 'To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product to exp