rees (excluding 0 and 180 degrees)\nx_values = np.linspace(0.01, 179.99, 10000)\ny_values = f(x_values)\n\n# Find the minimum and maximum values of y\ny_min = np.min(y_values)\ny_max = np.max(y_values)\n\nprint(((y_min, y_max)))\n```\n```output\n(-7.59913178467652, -1.2230748134070702e-06)\n```\nThe minimum value of \\( y = \\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x}) \\) is approximately \\(-7.60\\), which is very close to negative infinity, and the maximum value is approximately \\(-1.22 \\times 10^{-6}\\), which is very close to 0. This confirms our analytical solution.\n\nTherefore, the range of the function \\( y = \\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x}) \\) for \\( 0^\\circ < x < 180^\\circ \\) is \\(\\boxed{(-\\infty, 0]}\\).", "To determine the range of the function \\( y = \\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x}) \\) for \\( 0^\\circ < x < 180^\\circ \\), we need to analyze the behavior of the function over this interval.\n\n1. First, consider the range of \\(\\sin x\\) for \\(0^\\circ < x < 180^\\circ\\). The sine function reaches its maximum value of 1 at \\(90^\\circ\\) and its minimum value of 0 at \\(0^\\circ\\) and \\(180^\\circ\\) (although these endpoints are not included in the interval). Therefore, for \\(0^\\circ < x < 180^\\circ\\), the value of \\(\\sin x\\) ranges from \\(0\\) (exclusive) to \\(1\\) (inclusive).\n\n2. Next, consider the effect of taking the square root of \\(\\sin x\\). The square root function is defined for non-negative inputs and is increasing. Therefore, \\(\\sqrt{\\sin x}\\) will range from \\(0\\) (exclusive) to \\(1\\) (inclusive).\n\n3. Finally, consider the effect of taking the base-2 logarithm of \\(\\sqrt{\\sin x}\\). The logarithm function \\(\\log_2\\) is defined for positive inputs and is increasing. Therefore, \\(\\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x})\\) will range from \\(-\\infty\\) (exclusive) to \\(0\\) (inclusive), because \\(\\log_2 (0^+) = -\\infty\\) and \\(\\log_2 (1) = 0\\).\n\nThus, the range of \\( y = \\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x}) \\) for \\(0^\\circ < x < 180^\\