e area of the shaded region. Let's break down the problem step-by-step:\n\n1. **Calculate the area of the larger circle**: The radius of the larger circle is 2, so its area is \\( \\pi \\cdot 2^2 = 4\\pi \\).\n\n2. **Calculate the area of the smaller circle**: The radius of the smaller circle is 1, so its area is \\( \\pi \\cdot 1^2 = \\pi \\).\n\n3. **Calculate the area of the shaded region**: The shaded region is the area of the larger circle minus the area of the smaller circle plus the overlapping area. Since the total area of the shaded region is \\(\\frac{5}{12}\\) of the area of the larger circle, we can write:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of shaded region} = \\frac{5}{12} \\cdot 4\\pi = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determine the overlapping area**: The overlapping area can be calculated by subtracting the area of the shaded region from the area of the larger circle minus the area of the smaller circle. Let's denote the overlapping area as \\(A_{\\text{overlap}}\\). The area of the shaded region can also be written as:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of shaded region} = 4\\pi - \\pi - A_{\\text{overlap}} + A_{\\text{overlap}} = 3\\pi - A_{\\text{overlap}}\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   3\\pi - A_{\\text{overlap}} = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(A_{\\text{overlap}}\\):\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{overlap}} = 3\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{9\\pi - 5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{4\\pi}{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the angle \\(\\angle ADC\\)**: The overlapping area is a sector of the smaller circle minus a triangle. The area of the sector of the smaller circle with angle \\(\\theta\\) (in radians) is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1^2 \\cdot \\theta = \\frac{\\theta}{2}\\). The area of the triangle formed by the two radii and the chord is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sin(\\theta)}{2}\\). So the area of the overlapping region is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\theta}{2} - \\frac{\\sin(\\theta)}{2} = \\frac{4\\pi}{3}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[