ically and fits the constraints of the problem.\n\nTherefore, the measure of the angle \\(\\angle ADC\\) is \\(\\boxed{100}\\) degrees.", "The area of the larger circle is $\\pi r^2 = \\pi (2)^2 = 4\\pi$. The area of the shaded region is $\\frac5{12}$ of the area of the larger circle, so it is $\\frac5{12} \\cdot 4\\pi = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}$.\n\nThe area of the smaller circle is $\\pi r^2 = \\pi (1)^2 = \\pi$. The area of the shaded region is also equal to the area of the smaller circle minus the area of the triangle $ADC$. So, we have $\\pi - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\sin \\angle ADC = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}$.\n\nSimplifying, we get $\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\sin \\angle ADC = \\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\n\nTherefore, $\\sin \\angle ADC = \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\cdot 2 = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$.\n\nSince $\\sin \\angle ADC = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$, we have $\\angle ADC = \\sin^{-1} \\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) \\approx 100.53^\\circ$.\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle ADC$ is $\\boxed{100.53^\\circ}$.\n\nFind the characteristic polynomial of the matrix:\n$$\n\\left(\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\n-8 & 7 & -9 \\\\\n-9 & -2 & 1 \\\\\n6 & -8 & 9 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\right)\n$$\nTo find the characteristic polynomial of a matrix, we subtract a scalar multiple of the identity matrix from the matrix and then take the determinant. Let's denote the matrix as $M$ and the scalar as $\\lambda$. The characteristic polynomial is given by $| M - \\lambda I |$.\n\nFor the given matrix:\n$$\nM = \\left(\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\n-8 & 7 & -9 \\\\\n-9 & -2 & 1 \\\\\n6 & -8 & 9 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\right)\n$$\n\nThe characteristic polynomial is:\n$$\n| M - \\lambda I | = \\left|\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\n-8 - \\lambda & 7 & -9 \\\\\n-9 & -2 - \\lambda & 1 \\\\\n6 & -8 & 9 - \\lambda \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\right|\n$$\n\nLet's compute the determinant step by step:\n\n1. Swap rows 1 and 2 for easier manipulation:\n$$\n| M - \\lambda I | = -\\left|\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\n-9 & -\\lambda - 2 & 1 \\\\\n-\\lambda - 8 & 7 & -9 \\\\\n6 & -8 & 9 - \\lambda \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\right|\n$$\n\n2. Add $\\frac{1}{9}(-8 - \\lambda)$ times row 1 to row 2:\n$$\n| M - \\lambda I | = -\\left|\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\n-9 & -\\lambda - 2 & 1 \\\\\n0 & \\frac{1}{9}(\\lambda^2 + 10\\lambda + 79) & -\\frac{\\lambda}{9} - \\frac{89}{9} \\\\\n6 & -8 & 9 - \\lambda \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\right|\n$$\n\n3. Add $\\frac{2}{3}$ times row 1 to row 3:\n$$\n| M - \\lambda I | = -\\left|\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\n-9 & -\\lambda - 2 & 1 \\\\\n0 & \\frac{1}{9}(\\lambda^2 + 10\\lambda + 79) & -\\frac{\\lambda}{9} - \\frac{89}{9} \\\\\n0 & -\\frac{2}{3}(\\lambda + 14) & \\frac{29}{3} - \\lambda \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\right|\n$$\n\n4. Add $\\frac{6(\\lambda + 14)}{79 + 10\\lambda + \\lambda^2}$ times row 2 to row 3:\n$$\n| M - \\lambda I | = -\\left|\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\n-9 & -\\lambda - 2 & 1 \\\\\n0 & \\frac{1}{9}(\\lambda^2 + 10\\lambda + 79) & -\\frac{\\lambda}{9} - \\frac{89}{9} \\\\\n0 & 0 & \\frac{-\\lambda^3 - \\lambda^2 - 57\\lambda + 529}{\\lambda^2 + 10\\lambda + 79} \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\right|\n$$\n\nThe characteristic polynomial is the determinant of the upper triangular matrix:\n$$\np(\\lambda) = -\\left( \\frac{1}{9}(\\lambda^2 + 10\\lambda + 79) \\right) \\left( \\frac{-\\lambda^3 - \\lambda^2 - 57\\lambda + 529}{\\lambda^2 + 10\\lambda + 79} \\right)\n$$\n\nSimplifying the polynomial:\n$$\np(\\lambda) = -\\lambda^3 - \\lambda^2 - 57\\lambda + 529\n$$\n\nTherefore, the characteristic polynomial of the given matrix is:\n$$\np(\\lambda) = -\\lambda^3 - \\lambda^2 - 57\\lambda + 529\n$$\n\nFind the cross product of the following vectors:\n$\\vec{a} = \\left(\n\\begin{array}{c}\n4 \\\\\n-6 \\\\\n-7 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\right)$ and\n$\\vec{b} = \\left(\n\\begin{array}{c}\n-8 \\\\\n-3 \\\\\n-5 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\right)$\nThe cross product of two vectors $\\vec{a} = (a_1, a_2, a_3)$ and $\\vec{b} = (b_1, b_2, b_3)$ is defined as:\n\n$\\vec{a} \\times \\vec{b} = \\left(\n\\