fy the distinct values in the range of \\( f(x) \\).\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the function f(x)\ndef f(x):\n    return np.floor((-5/8)**x)\n\n# Generate a range of x values from 0 to a large number\nx_values = np.linspace(0, 100, 10000)\nf_values = f(x_values)\n\n# Find the distinct values in the range of f(x)\ndistinct_values = np.unique(f_values)\nprint(len(distinct_values))\n```\n```output\n2\n```\nThe output indicates that there are exactly 2 distinct values in the range of the function \\( f(x) = \\left\\lfloor \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^x \\right\\rfloor \\) for \\( x \\in [0, \\infty) \\). These values are 1 and 0.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct values in the range of \\( f(x) \\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to analyze the function \\( f(x) = \\left\\lfloor \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^x \\right\\rfloor \\) for \\( x \\in [0, \\infty) \\).\n\n1. **Understand the behavior of the function:**\n   - For \\( x = 0 \\), \\( \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^0 = 1 \\), so \\( f(0) = \\left\\lfloor 1 \\right\\rfloor = 1 \\).\n   - For \\( x > 0 \\), \\( \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^x \\) will be a negative number if \\( x \\) is odd and a positive number if \\( x \\) is even. However, since we are taking the floor function, we need to consider the absolute value of the expression and then take the floor.\n\n2. **Analyze the range of \\( \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^x \\):**\n   - For \\( x = 1 \\), \\( \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^1 = -\\frac{5}{8} \\), so \\( f(1) = \\left\\lfloor -\\frac{5}{8} \\right\\rfloor = -1 \\).\n   - For \\( x = 2 \\), \\( \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^2 = \\frac{25}{64} \\), so \\( f(2) = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{25}{64} \\right\\rfloor = 0 \\).\n   - For \\( x = 3 \\), \\( \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^3 = -\\frac{125}{512} \\), so \\( f(3) = \\left\\lfloor -\\frac{125}{512} \\right\\rfloor = -1 \\).\n   - For \\( x = 4 \\), \\( \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^4 = \\frac{625}{409