sqrt[3]{2} - 1)\n   \\]\n   Now, we need to express \\(\\sqrt[3]{2} - 1\\) as a continued fraction. Let's denote \\(y = \\sqrt[3]{2} - 1\\). Then:\n   \\[\n   y = \\sqrt[3]{2} - 1\n   \\]\n   Taking the reciprocal, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{y} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{2} - 1}\n   \\]\n   To rationalize the denominator, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by \\((\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{y} = \\frac{(\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1}{(\\sqrt[3]{2} - 1)(\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1} = \\frac{(\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1}{2 - 1} = (\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1\n   \\]\n   Now, we need to find the integer part of \\((\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1\\):\n   \\[\n   (\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1 \\approx 1.2599^2 + 1.2599 + 1 \\approx 3.7937\n   \\]\n   So, \\(b = 3\\). We can write:\n   \\[\n   (\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1 = 3 + (\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + \\sqrt[3]{2} - 2\n   \\]\n   Now, we need to express \\((\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + \\sqrt[3]{2} - 2\\) as a continued fraction. Let's denote \\(z = (\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + \\sqrt[3]{2} - 2\\). Then:\n   \\[\n   z = (\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + \\sqrt[3]{2} - 2\n   \\]\n   Taking the reciprocal, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{z} = \\frac{1}{(\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + \\sqrt[3]{2} - 2}\n   \\]\n   To rationalize the denominator, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by \\((\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 - \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{z} = \\frac{(\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 - \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1}{((\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + \\sqrt[3]{2} - 2)((\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 - \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1)} = \\frac{(\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 - \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1}{2 - 2\\sqrt[3]{2} + 1} = \\frac{(\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 - \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1}{3 - 2\\sqrt[3]{2}}\n   \\]\n   Multiplying the numerator and the denominator by \\(3 + 2\\sqrt[3]{2}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{z} = \\frac{((\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 - \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1)(3 + 2\\sqrt[3]{2})}{(3 - 2\\sqrt[3]{2})(3 + 2\\sqrt[3]{2})} = \\frac{3(\\sqrt[3]{2})^2 + 2(\\sqrt[3]{2})^3 - 3\\sqrt[3]{2} - 2(\\sqr