n identify a repeating pattern.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nF(1) &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF(2) &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF(3) &\\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF(4) &\\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF(5) &\\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF(6) &\\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 4 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF(7) &\\equiv 1 + 0 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF(8) &\\equiv 0 + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF(9) &\\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF(10) &\\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF(11) &\\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF(12) &\\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 4 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the sequence modulo 4 repeats every 6 terms: 1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 0. Therefore, to find the remainder when the 100th term is divided by 4, we need to find the position of the 100th term in the repeating cycle. Since the cycle length is 6, we compute \\(100 \\mod 6\\):\n\n\\[\n100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4.\n\\]\n\nSo, the 100th term has the same remainder as the 4th term in the cycle, which is 3. Thus, the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is 3. We can confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the nth Fibonacci number modulo m\ndef fibonacci_mod(n, m):\n    if n == 1 or n == 2:\n        return 1\n    a, b = 1, 1\n    for _ in range(3, n + 1):\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % m\n    return b\n\n# Find the 100th Fibonacci number modulo 4\nremainder = fibonacci_mod(100, 4)\nprint(remainder)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to find the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence and then determine the remainder when it is divided by 4. However, instead of calculating the 100th term directly, which can be computationally intensive, we can use the property of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4. The sequence will eventually repeat when considered