 by:\n\\[\n(n-1) \\cdot n \\cdot (n+1) = 120\n\\]\nThe mean of these three integers is:\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1) + n + (n+1)}{3} = \\frac{3n}{3} = n\n\\]\nAccording to the problem, the product of the three integers divided by the mean of the three integers is 24:\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1) \\cdot n \\cdot (n+1)}{n} = 24\n\\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[\n(n-1) \\cdot (n+1) = 24\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\nn^2 - 1 = 24\n\\]\nSo:\n\\[\nn^2 = 25\n\\]\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nn = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad n = -5\n\\]\nSince we are looking for positive integers, we take \\(n = 5\\). Therefore, the three consecutive integers are \\(4\\), \\(5\\), and \\(6\\). The largest of these three integers is \\(6\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Define the product of three consecutive integers\ndef product_of_consecutive_integers(n):\n    return (n-1) * n * (n+1)\n\n# Define the mean of three consecutive integers\ndef mean_of_consecutive_integers(n):\n    return n\n\n# Check the product and mean for n = 5\nn = 5\nproduct = product_of_consecutive_integers(n)\nmean = mean_of_consecutive_integers(n)\nresult = product // mean\n\n# Verify the conditions\nproduct_condition = (product == 120)\nmean_condition = (result == 24)\nlargest_integer = n + 1 if product_condition and mean_condition else None\n\nprint(largest_integer)\n```\n```output\n6\n```\nThe largest of the three consecutive integers is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).", "To solve this problem, let's denote the three consecutive integers by \\(n-1\\), \\(n\\), and \\(n+1\\). The product of these integers is given by:\n\n\\[\n(n-1) \\cdot n \\cdot (n+1) = 120\n\\]\n\nWe are also given that the product divided by the mean of the three integers is 24. The mean of the three integers is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1) + n + (n+1)}{3} = \\frac{3n}{3} = n\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1) \\cdot n \\cdot (n+1)}{n} = 24\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\n\\