). We can break this down into two separate inequalities and solve them step by step.\n\n1. Solve \\(\\frac{1}{4} < \\frac{x}{7}\\):\n\\[ \\frac{1}{4} < \\frac{x}{7} \\implies 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} < x \\implies \\frac{7}{4} < x \\implies 1.75 < x \\]\nSince \\( x \\) is an integer, \\( x \\) must be greater than 1.75, so \\( x \\geq 2 \\).\n\n2. Solve \\(\\frac{x}{7} < \\frac{1}{3}\\):\n\\[ \\frac{x}{7} < \\frac{1}{3} \\implies x < 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\implies x < \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x < 2.33 \\]\nSince \\( x \\) is an integer, \\( x \\) must be less than 2.33, so \\( x \\leq 2 \\).\n\nCombining these two results, we get:\n\\[ 2 \\leq x \\leq 2 \\]\nThis means the only integer \\( x \\) that satisfies both inequalities is \\( x = 2 \\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Define the range of possible x values based on the inequalities\npossible_x_values = [x for x in range(2, 3)]  # Since x must be an integer between 2 and 2\n\n# Verify the solution\nvalid_x = [x for x in possible_x_values if (1/4) < (x/7) < (1/3)]\n\nprint(valid_x)\n```\n```output\n[2]\n```\nThe integer \\( x \\) that satisfies the inequality \\(\\frac{1}{4} < \\frac{x}{7} < \\frac{1}{3}\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to find an integer \\( x \\) that satisfies the inequality \\(\\frac{1}{4} < \\frac{x}{7} < \\frac{1}{3}\\).\n\nFirst, we can split the compound inequality into two separate inequalities:\n\n1. \\(\\frac{1}{4} < \\frac{x}{7}\\)\n2. \\(\\frac{x}{7} < \\frac{1}{3}\\)\n\nWe can solve each inequality separately for \\( x \\):\n\n1. \\(\\frac{1}{4} < \\frac{x}{7}\\) implies \\( 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} < x \\), or \\( \\frac{7}{4} < x \\). Since \\( \\frac{7}{4} = 1.75 \\), we get \\( x > 1.75 \\). The smallest integer greater than 1.75 is 2.\n2. \\(\\frac{x}{7} < \\frac{1}{3}\\) implies \\( x < 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\), or \\( x < \\frac{7}{3} \\). Since \\( \\frac{7}{3} \\approx 2.333 \\), we get \\( x < 2.