 \\(60\\). The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{60}\n\\]\n\nGiven the function $f(x) = \\begin{cases} 1, & x\\geq 0 \\\\ -2, & x<0 \\end{cases}$, if $x_1$ and $x_2$ both satisfy the inequality $x+(x-1)f(x+1)\\leq 5$, then the maximum value of $x_1-x_2$ is \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_.\nTo solve the problem, we need to analyze the inequality \\(x + (x-1)f(x+1) \\leq 5\\) for the piecewise function \\(f(x)\\). The function \\(f(x)\\) is defined as follows:\n\\[ f(x) = \\begin{cases} \n1, & x \\geq 0 \\\\ \n-2, & x < 0 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nWe need to consider two cases for \\(f(x+1)\\):\n\n1. When \\(x+1 \\geq 0\\), i.e., \\(x \\geq -1\\), \\(f(x+1) = 1\\). The inequality becomes:\n\\[ x + (x-1) \\cdot 1 \\leq 5 \\implies x + x - 1 \\leq 5 \\implies 2x - 1 \\leq 5 \\implies 2x \\leq 6 \\implies x \\leq 3 \\]\nSo, for \\(x \\geq -1\\), the inequality holds when \\(-1 \\leq x \\leq 3\\).\n\n2. When \\(x+1 < 0\\), i.e., \\(x < -1\\), \\(f(x+1) = -2\\). The inequality becomes:\n\\[ x + (x-1) \\cdot (-2) \\leq 5 \\implies x - 2(x-1) \\leq 5 \\implies x - 2x + 2 \\leq 5 \\implies -x + 2 \\leq 5 \\implies -x \\leq 3 \\implies x \\geq -3 \\]\nSo, for \\(x < -1\\), the inequality holds when \\(-3 \\leq x < -1\\).\n\nCombining these two cases, the inequality \\(x + (x-1)f(x+1) \\leq 5\\) holds for \\(-3 \\leq x \\leq 3\\). The maximum value of \\(x_1 - x_2\\) will be the difference between the maximum and minimum values of \\(x\\) in this interval, which is \\(3 - (-3) = 6\\).\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of \\(x_1 - x_2\\) is \\(6\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the piecewise function f(x+1)\nf = sp.Piecewise((1, x + 1 >= 0), (-2, x + 1 < 0))\n\n# Define the inequality x + (x-1)*f(x+1) <= 5\ninequality = x + (x-1)*f - 5\n\n# Solve the inequality for the two cases\nsolution1 = sp.solve(inequality.subs(f, 1) <= 0, x)  # Case 1: f(x+1) = 1\nsolution2 = sp.solve(inequality.subs(f, -