ces) / sp.factorial(num_offices - num_people)\n\n# Print the result\nprint(num_ways)\n```\n```output\n60\n```\nThe number of ways to assign 3 people to 5 different offices, where each person gets exactly one office and no two people share an office, is \\(\\boxed{60}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of ways to assign 3 people to 5 different offices such that each person gets exactly one office and no two people share an office. This is a permutation problem where we are selecting 3 offices out of 5 and arranging 3 people in those offices.\n\nThe number of ways to choose 3 offices out of 5 is given by the permutation formula \\(P(n, k) = \\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}\\), where \\(n\\) is the total number of items to choose from, and \\(k\\) is the number of items to choose. In this case, \\(n = 5\\) and \\(k = 3\\).\n\nSo, the number of ways to choose 3 offices out of 5 is \\(P(5, 3) = \\frac{5!}{(5-3)!} = \\frac{5!}{2!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1}{2 \\times 1} = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 = 60\\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python using the sympy library to ensure the calculation is correct.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the number of offices and people\nn = 5\nk = 3\n\n# Calculate the number of permutations P(n, k)\nnum_ways = sp.perm(n, k)\n\n# Print the result\nprint(num_ways)\n```\n```output\nAttributeError: module 'sympy' has no attribute 'perm'\n```\nIt appears that there was an error because the `sympy` library does not have a `perm` function. Instead, we can use the factorial function to calculate the permutations manually. The permutation formula \\(P(n, k) = \\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}\\) can be implemented using the factorial function from `sympy`.\n\nLet's correct the code:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the number of offices and people\nn = 5\nk = 3\n\n# Calculate the number of permutations P(n, k) = n! / (n-k)!\nnum_ways = sp.factorial(n) / sp.factorial(n - k)\n\n# Print the result\nprint(num_way