 a polynomial equation in \\( x \\). Let's use Python to perform this calculation and find the polynomial.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, cos, acos, solve, Eq\n\n# Define the variable\nx = symbols('x')\n\n# Define the equation arccos(x) + arccos(2x) + arccos(3x) = pi\nalpha = acos(x)\nbeta = acos(2*x)\ngamma = acos(3*x)\nequation = Eq(alpha + beta + gamma, 3.141592653589793)\n\n# Solve the equation for x\nsolutions = solve(equation, x)\nprint(solutions)\n```\n```output\nNo algorithms are implemented to solve equation acos(x) + acos(2*x) + acos(3*x) - pi\n```\nIt seems that directly solving the equation \\(\\arccos(x) + \\arccos(2x) + \\arccos(3x) = \\pi\\) symbolically is not feasible due to the complexity of the inverse trigonometric functions. Instead, we will derive the polynomial equation step-by-step by considering the trigonometric identities and then solve it numerically.\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma) = -1,\n\\]\nwe substitute \\(\\alpha = \\arccos(x)\\), \\(\\beta = \\arccos(2x)\\), and \\(\\gamma = \\arccos(3x)\\) into the cosine addition formula:\n\\[\n\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma) = \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\cos \\gamma - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\beta \\sin \\gamma - \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\sin \\gamma - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta \\cos \\gamma.\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(\\cos \\alpha = x\\), \\(\\cos \\beta = 2x\\), and \\(\\cos \\gamma = 3x\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma) = x \\cdot 2x \\cdot 3x - x \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - (2x)^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - (3x)^2} - \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\cdot 2x \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - (3x)^2} - \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - (2x)^2} \\cdot 3x.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we have:\n\\[\n-1 = 6x^3 - x \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - 9x^2} - 2x \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - 9x^2} - 3x \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2}.\n\\]\n\nWe will now square both sides to eliminate the square roots and obtain a polynomial equation. Let's proceed with this calculation in Python.\n``