 largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the following system of congruences:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nSince the last two congruences have the same remainder, we can combine them to get:\n\\[\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143}\n\\]\nwhere 143 is the least common multiple of 11 and 13. Now we need to find an integer \\(x\\) that satisfies both:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nWe can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem to solve this system. Let's denote the solution by \\(x = 143k + 10\\). We need to find \\(k\\) such that:\n\\[\n143k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[\n143k + 10 \\equiv 6k + 3 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSo the equation becomes:\n\\[\n6k + 3 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSimplifying further, we get:\n\\[\n6k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nTo solve for \\(k\\), we need to find the modular inverse of 6 modulo 7. The modular inverse of 6 modulo 7 is 6, because \\(6 \\cdot 6 = 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\). So we multiply both sides by 6:\n\\[\nk \\equiv 2 \\cdot 6 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\\[\nk \\equiv 12 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\\[\nk \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSo \\(k = 7m + 5\\) for some integer \\(m\\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\(x\\), we get:\n\\[\nx = 143(7m + 5) + 10 = 1001m + 725\n\\]\nTo find the largest \\(x\\) less than 2010, we need to find the largest \\(m\\) such that:\n\\[\n1001m + 725 < 2010\n\\]\nSolving for \\(m\\), we get:\n\\[\n1001m < 1285\n\\]\n\\[\nm < \\frac{1285}{1001}\n\\]\n\\[\nm < 1.283\n\\]\nSo the largest integer \\(m\\) that satisfies this inequality is \\(m = 1\\). Substituting \\(m = 1\\) into the expression for \\(x\\), we get:\n\\[\nx = 1001 \\cdot 1 + 725 = 1726\n\\]\nTherefore, the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the given conditions is 1726. Let's verify this using Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Functio