are.\n4. Calculate the length of the diagonal of the square.\n\nLet's start by finding the side length of the equilateral triangle. The formula for the area of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(a\\) is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} a^2\n\\]\nGiven that the area is \\(16\\sqrt{3}\\) square centimeters, we can set up the equation:\n\\[\n16\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} a^2\n\\]\nSolving for \\(a\\):\n\\[\n16\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} a^2 \\implies 16 = \\frac{1}{4} a^2 \\implies a^2 = 64 \\implies a = 8\n\\]\nSo, the side length of the equilateral triangle is 8 centimeters. The perimeter of the equilateral triangle is:\n\\[\n3 \\times 8 = 24 \\text{ centimeters}\n\\]\nSince the square has the same perimeter as the equilateral triangle, the perimeter of the square is also 24 centimeters. The side length of the square \\(s\\) is:\n\\[\n4s = 24 \\implies s = 6 \\text{ centimeters}\n\\]\nThe length of the diagonal \\(d\\) of the square can be found using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nd = s\\sqrt{2} = 6\\sqrt{2} \\text{ centimeters}\n\\]\nSo, the length of the diagonal of the square is \\(6\\sqrt{2}\\) centimeters. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Given area of the equilateral triangle\narea_triangle = 16 * math.sqrt(3)\n\n# Calculate the side length of the equilateral triangle\na = math.sqrt(4 * area_triangle / math.sqrt(3))\n\n# Perimeter of the equilateral triangle\nperimeter_triangle = 3 * a\n\n# Side length of the square\nside_square = perimeter_triangle / 4\n\n# Length of the diagonal of the square\ndiagonal_square = side_square * math.sqrt(2)\n\nprint(diagonal_square)\n```\n```output\n8.485281374238571\n```\nThe length of the diagonal of the square is \\(6\\sqrt{2}\\) centimeters, which is approximately 8.485 centimeters. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the side length of the equilateral triangle usi