 \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n  \\n\n ', "To determine the cost of a 3-inch cube, we first need to understand the cost structure. The cost of a cube is the sum of the cost of the concrete and the cost of the gold paint. The cost of the concrete is proportional to the volume of the cube, and the cost of the gold paint is proportional to the surface area of the cube.\n\nLet's denote:\n- $k_1$ as the cost per cubic inch of concrete.\n- $k_2$ as the cost per square inch of gold paint.\n\nFor a cube of side length $s$:\n- The volume of the cube is $s^3$.\n- The surface area of the cube is $6s^2$.\n\nSo, the total cost $C(s)$ of a cube of side length $s$ is given by:\n\\[ C(s) = k_1 \\cdot s^3 + k_2 \\cdot 6s^2 \\]\n\nGiven:\n- The cost of a 1-inch cube is \\$1.30.\n- The cost of a 2-inch cube is \\$6.80.\n\nWe can set up the following system of equations:\n\\[ C(1) = k_1 \\cdot 1^3 + k_2 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 1^2 = k_1 + 6k_2 = 1.30 \\]\n\\[ C(2) = k_1 \\cdot 2^3 + k_2 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 2^2 = 8k_1 + 24k_2 = 6.80 \\]\n\nWe can solve this system of equations to find $k_1$ and $k_2$.\n\nFirst, we solve the first equation for $k_1$:\n\\[ k_1 = 1.30 - 6k_2 \\]\n\nNext, we substitute this expression for $k_1$ into the second equation:\n\\[ 8(1.30 - 6k_2) + 24k_2 = 6.80 \\]\n\\[ 10.40 - 48k_2 + 24k_2 = 6.80 \\]\n\\[ 10.40 - 24k_2 = 6.80 \\]\n\\[ -24k_2 = 6.80 - 10.40 \\]\n\\[ -24k_2 = -3.60 \\]\n\\[ k_2 = \\frac{3.60}{24} = 0.15 \\]\n\nNow, we substitute $k_2$ back into the equation for $k_1$:\n\\[ k_1 = 1.30 - 6(0.15) = 1.30 - 0.90 = 0.40 \\]\n\nSo, the cost per cubic inch of concrete is \\$0.40 and the cost per square inch of gold paint is \\$0.15. Now we can find the cost of a 3-inch cube:\n\\[ C(3) = k_1 \\cdot 3^3 + k_2 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 3^2 = 0.40 \\cdot 27 + 0.15 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 9 = 10.80 + 8.10 = 18.90 \\]\n\nThe cost of a 3-inch cube is \\(\\boxed{18.90}\\).", 'The sum of the first $n$ terms of the sequence $1,1,2,3,5,\\cdots$ is $S_n$.  What is the remainder when $S_{2006}$ is divided by $5$?  (Hint:  The sequence is the Fibonacci sequence, with the $n^{th}$ term equal to the sum of the two terms before it.  $S_1=1$, $S_2=2$, $S_3=4$, $S_4=7$, $S_5=12$, etc.)  \\boxed{} \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \