text{(Equation 1)} \\]\n\nFor a 2-inch cube:\n\\[ 6.80 = c \\cdot 2^3 + p \\cdot 6 \\cdot 2^2 \\]\n\\[ 6.80 = 8c + 24p \\quad \\text{(Equation 2)} \\]\n\nWe now have a system of linear equations:\n1. $c + 6p = 1.30$\n2. $8c + 24p = 6.80$\n\nWe can solve this system of equations to find the values of $c$ and $p$. Let's start by solving Equation 1 for $c$:\n\\[ c = 1.30 - 6p \\]\n\nSubstitute this into Equation 2:\n\\[ 8(1.30 - 6p) + 24p = 6.80 \\]\n\\[ 10.40 - 48p + 24p = 6.80 \\]\n\\[ 10.40 - 24p = 6.80 \\]\n\\[ -24p = 6.80 - 10.40 \\]\n\\[ -24p = -3.60 \\]\n\\[ p = \\frac{3.60}{24} = 0.15 \\]\n\nNow substitute $p = 0.15$ back into Equation 1 to find $c$:\n\\[ c + 6(0.15) = 1.30 \\]\n\\[ c + 0.90 = 1.30 \\]\n\\[ c = 1.30 - 0.90 = 0.40 \\]\n\nSo the cost of the concrete per cubic inch is $0.40 and the cost of the gold paint per square inch is $0.15. \n\nNow we can find the cost of a 3-inch cube:\n\\[ \\text{Cost} = c \\cdot 3^3 + p \\cdot 6 \\cdot 3^2 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Cost} = 0.40 \\cdot 27 + 0.15 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 9 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Cost} = 10.80 + 8.10 = 18.90 \\]\n\nTherefore, the cost of a 3-inch cube is $\\boxed{18.90}$. \n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the cost components\r\nc = 0.40  # cost of concrete per cubic inch\r\np = 0.15  # cost of gold paint per square inch\r\n\r\n# Calculate the cost of a 3-inch cube\r\nside_length = 3\r\nvolume = side_length ** 3\r\nsurface_area = 6 * (side_length ** 2)\r\ncost_3_inch_cube = c * volume + p * surface_area\r\nprint(cost_3_inch_cube)\n```", 'Let $a_n$ be the $n$th term of a sequence.  If $a_1 = 1$ and $a_n = 2a_{n - 1} + 1$ for all $n > 1$, then what is the value of $a_5$? \\boxed{}. Shaynuser\nLet $x$ be a real number.  If $\\frac{x^2 + 5x + 6}{x + 3} = x + 2$, then what is the value of $x^2 + 5x + 6$? Shaynassistant\nLet $a$, $b$, and $c$ be real numbers such that $a + b + c = 10$, $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = 20$, and $a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = 30$.  Then what is the value of $a^4 + b^4 + c^