y checking the other rows, columns, and diagonals with \\( n = 7 \\):\n- Sum of the first row: \\( (7-3) + 3 + (7+1) = 4 + 3 + 8 = 15 \\)\n- Sum of the second row: \\( (7+2) + (2 \\cdot 7 - 9) + 1 = 9 + 5 + 1 = 15 \\)\n- Sum of the third row: \\( 2 + 7 + (7-1) = 2 + 7 + 6 = 15 \\)\n- Sum of the first column: \\( (7-3) + (7+2) + 2 = 4 + 9 + 2 = 15 \\)\n- Sum of the second column: \\( 3 + (2 \\cdot 7 - 9) + 7 = 3 + 5 + 7 = 15 \\)\n- Sum of the third column: \\( (7+1) + 1 + (7-1) = 8 + 1 + 6 = 15 \\)\n- Sum of the main diagonal: \\( (7-3) + (2 \\cdot 7 - 9) + (7-1) = 4 + 5 + 6 = 15 \\)\n- Sum of the other diagonal: \\( (7+1) + (2 \\cdot 7 - 9) + 2 = 8 + 5 + 2 = 15 \\)\n\nSince all the sums are equal to 15, the value of \\( n \\) is indeed correct. Therefore, the value of \\( n \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to use the properties of a magic square. In a 3x3 magic square, the sum of the numbers in each row, each column, and both main diagonals is the same. Let's denote this common sum by $S$.\n\nGiven the magic square:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\nn-3 & 3 & n+1 \\\\\n\\hline\nn+2 & 2n-9 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & n & n-1 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nWe can write the following equations for the sum of each row, column, and diagonal:\n\n1. Sum of the first row: $(n-3) + 3 + (n+1) = S$\n2. Sum of the second row: $(n+2) + (2n-9) + 1 = S$\n3. Sum of the third row: $2 + n + (n-1) = S$\n4. Sum of the first column: $(n-3) + (n+2) + 2 = S$\n5. Sum of the second column: $3 + (2n-9) + n = S$\n6. Sum of the third column: $(n+1) + 1 + (n-1) = S$\n7. Sum of the main diagonal: $(n-3) + (2n-9) + (n-1) = S$\n8. Sum of the other diagonal: $(n+1) + (2n-9) + 2 = S$\n\nWe can start by solving the first row equation:\n\n\\[\n(n-3) + 3 + (n+1) = S \\implies 2n + 1 = S\n\\]\n\nNext, we can use the second row equation:\n\n\\[\n(n+2) + (2n-9) + 1 = S \\implies 3n - 6 = S\n\\]\n\nSince both expressions equal $S$, we can set them equal to each other: