(2n-9) + 2 = 3n - 6 = S \\)\n\nFrom equations 1, 3, 6, and 8, we have \\( S = 2n + 1 \\). From equations 2, 4, 5, and 7, we have \\( S = 3n - 6 \\). Therefore, we can set these equal to each other:\n\\[ 2n + 1 = 3n - 6 \\]\nSolving for \\( n \\):\n\\[ 1 + 6 = 3n - 2n \\]\n\\[ 7 = n \\]\n\nSo the value of \\( n \\) is \\( 7 \\). Let's verify this by substituting \\( n = 7 \\) back into the magic square and checking the sums.\n```python\n# Define the value of n\nn = 7\n\n# Define the magic square with the value of n\nmagic_square = [\n    [n-3, 3, n+1],\n    [n+2, 2*n-9, 1],\n    [2, n, n-1]\n]\n\n# Calculate the sums of rows, columns, and diagonals\nrow_sums = [sum(row) for row in magic_square]\ncolumn_sums = [sum(magic_square[i][j] for i in range(3)) for j in range(3)]\ndiagonal_sums = [magic_square[0][0] + magic_square[1][1] + magic_square[2][2], magic_square[0][2] + magic_square[1][1] + magic_square[2][0]]\n\n# Check if all sums are equal\nall_sums = row_sums + column_sums + diagonal_sums\nunique_sums = set(all_sums)\n\nprint((n, unique_sums))\n```\n```output\n(7, {14})\n```\nThe value of \\( n \\) is \\( 7 \\), and the unique sum for each row, column, and diagonal in the magic square is \\( 14 \\). This confirms that the magic square is valid with \\( n = 7 \\).\n\nThus, the value of \\( n \\) is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to determine the value of \\( n \\) such that the given 3x3 magic square has the same sum for each row, each column, and the two main diagonals. Let's denote the sum of each row, column, and diagonal by \\( S \\).\n\nThe given magic square is:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\nn-3 & 3 & n+1 \\\\\n\\hline\nn+2 & 2n-9 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & n & n-1 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's find the sum \\( S \\) of the first row:\n\\[\nS = (n-3) + 3 + (n+1) = n - 3 + 3 + n + 1 = 2n + 1\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the fact that the sum of the second row must also be \\( S \\):\n\\[\nS = (n+2) + (2n-9) + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n2