(\\frac{1}{6}\\), we have \\(p > \\frac{1}{6}\\) and \\(q < \\frac{1}{6}\\). The sum of all probabilities must be 1, so we have:\n   \\[\n   p + q + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} = 1 \\implies p + q + \\frac{2}{3} = 1 \\implies p + q = \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(p > \\frac{1}{6}\\) and \\(q < \\frac{1}{6}\\), let's assume \\(p = \\frac{1}{3} - q\\). We need to find the values of \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) that satisfy these conditions.\n\n4. **Probability of obtaining a sum of 7**: When two such dice are rolled, the probability of obtaining a sum of 7 can be calculated by considering all possible pairs of faces that sum to 7. The pairs are \\((1,6)\\), \\((2,5)\\), \\((3,4)\\), \\((4,3)\\), \\((5,2)\\), and \\((6,1)\\). The probability of obtaining each of these pairs is the product of the probabilities of obtaining each face. Therefore, the probability of obtaining a sum of 7 is:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{sum of 7}) = 2(pq + \\frac{1}{36} + \\frac{1}{36}) = 2(pq + \\frac{1}{18}) = 2pq + \\frac{1}{9}\n   \\]\n   We are given that this probability is \\(\\frac{47}{288}\\), so we have:\n   \\[\n   2pq + \\frac{1}{9} = \\frac{47}{288}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   2pq = \\frac{47}{288} - \\frac{1}{9} = \\frac{47}{288} - \\frac{32}{288} = \\frac{15}{288} = \\frac{5}{96}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\(pq = \\frac{5}{192}\\).\n\n5. **Solve for \\(p\\) and \\(q\\)**: We have \\(p + q = \\frac{1}{3}\\) and \\(pq = \\frac{5}{192}\\). These are the sum and product of the roots of a quadratic equation. The quadratic equation whose roots are \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) is:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)x + \\frac{5}{192} = 0\n   \\]\n   Solving this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{\\frac{1}{3} \\pm \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - 4 \\cdot \\frac{5}{192}}}{2} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{3} \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9} - \\frac{5}{48}}}{2} = \\frac{\\fr