n(route) - 1):\n        total_distance += distances[(route[i], route[i + 1])]\n    distances_sum.append(total_distance)\n\n# Find the minimum distance\nmin_distance = min(distances_sum)\nprint(min_distance)\n```\n```output\n14\n```\nThe shortest distance that can be traveled starting from any point \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), or \\(D\\) and visiting the other three points exactly once is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).", "To find the shortest distance that can be traveled starting from any point and visiting the other three points exactly once, we need to consider all possible routes and calculate their total distances. This is essentially finding the shortest Hamiltonian path in the graph.\n\nThe points and their distances are:\n- $A$ to $B$: 6\n- $A$ to $C$: 6\n- $A$ to $D$: 5\n- $B$ to $C$: 5\n- $B$ to $D$: 3\n- $C$ to $D$: 4\n\nWe will calculate the total distance for each possible route starting from each point and visiting the other three points exactly once. There are 3! = 6 possible routes for each starting point, but since we are looking for the shortest distance, we can limit our calculations to the unique routes.\n\nLet's calculate the distances for each possible route:\n\n1. Starting from $A$:\n   - $A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D$: $6 + 5 + 4 = 15$\n   - $A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C$: $6 + 3 + 4 = 13$\n   - $A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D$: $6 + 5 + 3 = 14$\n   - $A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B$: $6 + 4 + 3 = 13$\n   - $A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C$: $5 + 3 + 5 = 13$\n   - $A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B$: $5 + 4 + 5 = 14$\n\n2. Starting from $B$:\n   - $B \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D$: $6 + 6 + 4 = 16$\n   - $B \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C$: $6 + 5 + 4 = 15$\n   - $B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow D$: $5 + 6 + 5 = 16$\n   - $B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow A$: $5 + 4 + 5 = 14$\n   - $B \\rightarrow D \\rightarr