d calculate their total distances:\n\n1. Starting at \\(A\\):\n   - \\(A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D\\)\n   - \\(A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C\\)\n   - \\(A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D\\)\n   - \\(A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B\\)\n   - \\(A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C\\)\n   - \\(A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B\\)\n\n2. Starting at \\(B\\):\n   - \\(B \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D\\)\n   - \\(B \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C\\)\n   - \\(B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow D\\)\n   - \\(B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow A\\)\n   - \\(B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow C\\)\n   - \\(B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow A\\)\n\n3. Starting at \\(C\\):\n   - \\(C \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D\\)\n   - \\(C \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B\\)\n   - \\(C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow D\\)\n   - \\(C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow A\\)\n   - \\(C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow B\\)\n   - \\(C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow A\\)\n\n4. Starting at \\(D\\):\n   - \\(D \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C\\)\n   - \\(D \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B\\)\n   - \\(D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow C\\)\n   - \\(D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow A\\)\n   - \\(D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow B\\)\n   - \\(D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow A\\)\n\nNow let's calculate the total distance for each path using the given distances:\n\n- \\(A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D\\): \\(3 + 4 + 5 = 12\\)\n- \\(A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C\\): \\(3 + 5 + 4 = 12\\)\n- \\(A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D\\): \\(6 + 4 + 5 = 15\\)\n- \\(A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B\\): \\(6 + 5 + 5 = 16\\)\n- \\(A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C\\): \\(5 + 5 + 4 = 14\\)\n- \\(A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B\\): \\(5 + 4 + 4 = 13\\)\n\n- \\(B \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D\\): \\(3 + 6 + 5 = 14\\)\n- \\(B \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C\\): \\(3 + 5 + 4 = 12\\)\n- \\(B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow D\\): \\(4 + 6 + 5 = 15\\)\n- \\(B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow A\\): \\(4 + 5 + 5 = 14\\)\n- \\(B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow C\\): \\(5 + 5 + 6 = 16\\)\n- \\(B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow A\\): \\(5 + 4 + 6 = 15\\)\n\n- \\(C \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D\\): \\(6 + 3 + 5 = 14\\)\n- \\(C \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B\\): \\(6 + 5 + 5 = 16\\)\n- \\(C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow D\\): \\(4 + 3 + 5 = 12\\)\n- \\(C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow A\\): \\(4 + 5 + 5 = 14\\)\n- \\(C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow B\\): \\(5 + 5 + 3 = 13\\)\n- \\(C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow A\\): \\(5 + 5 + 3 = 13\\)\n\n- \\(D \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C\\): \\(5 + 3 + 4 = 12\\)\n- \\(D \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B\\): \\(5 + 6 + 4 = 15\\)\n- \\(D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow C\\): \\(5 + 3 + 6 = 14\\)\n- \\(D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow A\\): \\(5 + 4 + 6 = 15\\)\n- \\(D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow B\\): \\(4 + 6 + 3 = 13\\)\n- \\(D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow A\\): \\(4 + 4 + 3 = 11\\)\n\nThe shortest distance among all these paths is 11, which occurs for the path \\(D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow A\\).\n\nSo the shortest distance that can be travelled is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).", "To find the shortest distance that can be traveled starting from any point and visiting the other three points exactly once, we need to consider all possible routes and calculate their total distances. The points and distances are given as follows:\n\n- \\(A\\) to \\(B\\) = 6\n- \\(A\\) to \\(C\\) = 6\n- \\(A\\) to \\(D\\) = 5\n- \\(B\\) to \\(C\\) = 5\n- \\(B\\) to \\(D\\) = 3\n- \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) = 4\n\nWe will calculate the total distance for each possible route starting from each point \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), and \\(D\\):\n\n1. Starting from \\(A\\):\n   - \\(A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D\\): \\(6 + 5 + 4 = 15\\)\n   - \\(A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C\\): \\(6 + 3 + 4 = 13\\)\n   - \\(A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D\\): \\(6 + 5 + 3 = 14\\)\n   - \\(A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B\\): \\(6 + 4 + 3 = 13\\)\n   - \\(A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C\\): \\(5 + 3 + 5 = 13\\)\n   - \\(A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B\\): \\(5 + 4 + 5 = 14\\)\n\n2. Starting from \\(B\\):\n   - \\(B \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D\\): \\(6 + 6 + 4 = 16\\)\n   - \\(B \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C\\): \\(6 + 5 + 4 = 15\\)\n   - \\(B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow D\\): \\(5 + 6 + 5 = 16\\)\n   - \\(B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow A\\): \\(5 + 4 + 5 = 14\\)\n   - \\(B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow C\\): \\(3 + 5 + 6 = 14\\)\n   - \\(B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow A\\): \\(3 + 4 + 6 = 13\\)\n\n3. Starting from \\(C\\):\n   - \\(C \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D\\): \\(6 + 6 + 3 = 15\\)\n   - \\(C \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B\\): \\(6 + 5 + 3 = 14\\)\n   - \\(C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow D\\): \\(5 + 6 + 5 = 16\\)\n   - \\(C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow A\\): \\(5 + 3 + 5 = 13\\)\n   - \\(C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow B\\): \\(4 + 5 + 6 = 15\\)\n   - \\(C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow A\\): \\(4 + 3 + 6 = 13\\)\n\n4. Starting from \\(D\\):\n   - \\(D \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C\\): \\(5 + 6 + 5 = 16\\)\n   - \\(D \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B\\): \\(5 + 6 + 5 = 16\\)\n   - \\(D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow C\\): \\(3 + 6 + 6 = 15\\)\n   - \\(D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow A\\): \\(3 + 5 + 6 = 14\\)\n   - \\(D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow A \\rightarrow B\\): \\(4 + 6 + 6 = 16\\)\n   - \\(D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow A\\): \\(4 + 5 + 6 = 15\\)\n\nThe shortest distance among all these routes is 13. Therefore, the shortest distance that can be traveled starting from any point and visiting the other three points exactly once is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the distances between the points\ndistances = {\n    ('A', 'B'): 6, ('A', 'C'): 6, ('A', 'D'): 5,\n    ('B', 'C'): 5, ('B', 'D'): 3,\n    ('C', 'D'): 4\n}\n\n# List of points\npoints = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D']\n\n# Function to calculate the total distance of a route\ndef calculate_distance(route):\n    total_distance = 0\n    for i in range(len(route) - 1):\n        total_distance += distances[(route[i], route[i + 1])]\n    return total_distance\n\n# Generate all possible routes starting from each point\nfrom itertools import permutations\n\nshortest_distance = float('inf')\nfor start in points:\n    for route in permutations(points[points.index(start) + 1:]):\n        full_route = (start,) + route\n        distance = calculate_distance(full_route)\n        if distance < shortest_distance:\n            shortest_distance = distance\n\nprint(shortest_distance)\n```", "To find the shortest distance that can be traveled starting from any point \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), or \\(D\\) and visiting the other three points exactly once, we need to calculate the total distance for each possible route and find the minimum among them. This is essentially finding the shortest Hamiltonian cycle in a weighted graph.\n\nLet's list all possible routes and calculate their total distances:\n\n1. Starting from \\(A\\):\n   - \\(A \\to B \\to C \\to D \\to A\\): \\(6 + 4 + 5 + 5 = 20\\)\n   - \\(A \\to B \\to D \\to C \\to A\\): \\(6 + 5 + 4 + 6 = 21\\)\n   - \\(A \\to C \\to B \\to D \\to A\\): \\(6 + 4 + 5 + 5 = 20\\)\n   - \\(A \\to C \\to D \\to B \\to A\\): \\(6 + 5 + 5 + 6 = 22\\)\n   - \\(A \\to D \\to B \\to C \\to A\\): \\(5 + 5 + 4 + 6 = 20\\)\n   - \\(A \\to D \\to C \\to B \\to A\\): \\(5 + 5 + 4 + 6 = 20\\)\n\n2. Starting from \\(B\\):\n   - \\(B \\to A \\to C \\to D \\to B\\): \\(6 + 6 + 5 + 5 = 22\\)\n   - \\(B \\to A \\to D \\to C \\to B\\): \\(6 + 5 + 5 + 4 = 20\\)\n   - \\(B \\to C \\to A \\to D \\to B\\): \\(4 + 6 + 5 + 5 = 20\\)\n   - \\(B \\to C \\to D \\to A \\to B\\): \\(4 + 5 + 5 + 6 = 20\\)\n   - \\(B \\to D \\to A \\to C \\to B\\): \\(5 + 5 + 6 + 4 = 20\\)\n   - \\(B \\to D \\to C \\to A \\to B\\): \\(5 + 5 + 6 + 6 = 22\\)\n\n3. Starting from \\(C\\):\n   - \\(C \\to A \\to B \\to D \\to C\\): \\(6 + 6 + 5 + 4 = 21\\)\n   - \\(C \\to A \\to D \\to B \\to C\\): \\(6 + 5 + 5 + 4 = 20\\)\n   - \\(C \\to B \\to A \\to D \\to C\\): \\(4 + 6 + 5 + 4 = 19\\)\n   - \\(C \\to B \\to D \\to A \\to C\\): \\(4 + 5 + 5 + 6 = 20\\)\n   - \\(C \\to D \\to A \\to B \\to C\\): \\(4 + 5 + 6 + 4 = 19\\)\n   - \\(C \\to D \\to B \\to A \\to C\\): \\(4 + 5 + 6 + 6 = 21\\)\n\n4. Starting from \\(D\\):\n   - \\(D \\to A \\to B \\to C \\to D\\): \\(5 + 6 + 4 + 4 = 19\\)\n   - \\(D \\to A \\to C \\to B \\to D\\): \\(5 + 6 + 4 + 5 = 20\\)\n   - \\(D \\to B \\to A \\to C \\to D\\): \\(5 + 6 + 6 + 4 = 21\\)\n   - \\(D \\to B \\to C \\to A \\to D\\): \\(5 + 4 + 6 + 5 = 20\\)\n   - \\(D \\to C \\to A \\to B \\to D\\): \\(4 + 6 + 6 + 5 = 21\\)\n   - \\(D \\to C \\to B \\to A \\to D\\): \\(4 + 4 + 6 + 5 = 19\\)\n\nFrom the calculations, the shortest distance is 19, which occurs for the routes \\(C \\to B \\to A \\to D \\to C\\), \\(C \\to D \\to A \\to B \\to C\\), \\(D \\to A \\to B \\to C \\to D\\), and \\(D \\to C \\to B \\to A \\to D\\).\n\nSo the shortest distance that can be travelled is \\(\\boxed{19}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the side length of the cube from which the steel sphere with a 3-inch radius is made. The sphere is formed by removing metal from the corners of the cube, which means the sphere's diameter is equal to the side length of the cube. Since the radius of the sphere is 3 inches, the diameter of the sphere is \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\) inches. Therefore, the side length of the cube is 6 inches.\n\nThe volume \\(V\\) of a cube with side length \\(s\\) is given by the formula:\n\\[ V = s^3 \\]\nIn this case, the side length \\(s\\) is 6 inches. So, the volume of the cube is:\n\\[ V = 6^3 = 216 \\text{ cubic inches} \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the side length of the cube\nside_length = 6\n\n# Calculate the volume of the cube\nvolume_cube = side_length ** 3\nprint(volume_cube)\n```\n```output\n216\n```\nThe volume of the cube is \\(\\boxed{216}\\) cubic inches.", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the side length of the cube that has the shortest possible side lengths while still allowing a steel sphere with a 3-inch radius to fit inside it. The sphere will touch the cube at the center of each face, meaning the diameter of the sphere is equal to the side length of the cube. Since the radius of the sphere is 3 inches, the diameter is \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\) inches. Therefore, the side length of the cube is 6 inches.\n\nThe volume \\(V\\) of a cube with side length \\(s\\) is given by the formula:\n\\[ V = s^3 \\]\n\nIn this case, the side length \\(s\\) is 6 inches. So, the volume of the cube is:\n\\[ V = 6^3 = 216 \\text{ cubic inches} \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the side length of the cube\nside_length = 6\n\n# Calculate the volume of the cube\nvolume_cube = side_length ** 3\nprint(volume_cube)\n```\n```output\n216\n```\nThe volume of the cube is \\(\\boxed{216}\\) cubic inches.", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the side length of the cube that has the shortest possible side lengths while still being able to fit a sphere of 3-inch radius inside it. The sphere will touch all six faces of the cube when it is inscribed inside the cube. Therefore, the diameter of the sphere will be equal to the side length of the cube. Since the radius of the sphere is 3 inches, the diameter of the sphere will be \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\) inches. This means the side length of the cube is 6 inches.\n\nThe volume \\(V\\) of a cube with side length \\(s\\) is given by the formula \\(V = s^3\\). Substituting \\(s = 6\\) into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[ V = 6^3 = 216 \\]\n\nSo, the volume of the cube is \\(216\\) cubic inches. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the side length of the cube\nside_length = 2 * 3  # since the radius of the sphere is 3 inches, the diameter (which is the side length of the cube) is 6 inches\n\n# Calculate the volume of the cube\nvolume_cube = side_length ** 3\nprint(volume_cube)\n```\n```output\n216\n```\nThe volume of the cube is \\(\\boxed{216}\\) cubic inches.", 'The volume of a sphere is given by the formula V = (4/3)πr^3, where r is the radius of the sphere. In this case, r = 3 inches, so the volume of the sphere is V = (4/3)π(3)^3 = 36π cubic inches.\n\nThe volume of a cube is given by the formula V = s^3, where s is the length of a side of the cube. Since the sphere is made by removing metal from the corners of the cube, the side length of the cube must be equal to the diameter of the sphere, which is 2r = 6 inches. Therefore, the volume of the cube is V = 6^3 = 216 cubic inches.\n\nThe volume of the metal removed from the cube is equal to the volume of the sphere, so the volume of the cube is equal to the volume of the metal removed plus the volume of the sphere. Therefore, the volume of the cube is V = 216 + 36π cubic inches.', "To solve this problem, we need to determine the side length of the cube from which the steel sphere of radius 3 inches is made by removing metal from the corners of the cube. The sphere is inscribed inside the cube, meaning that the diameter of the sphere is equal to the side length of the cube.\n\n1. The radius of the sphere is 3 inches, so the diameter of the sphere is \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\) inches.\n2. Since the sphere is inscribed in the cube, the side length of the cube is equal to the diameter of the sphere, which is 6 inches.\n3. The volume of the cube is given by the formula \\( \\text{side length}^3 \\).\n\nLet's calculate the volume of the cube using Python code.\n```python\n# Side length of the cube\nside_length = 6\n\n# Volume of the cube\nvolume_cube = side_length ** 3\nprint(volume_cube)\n```\n```output\n216\n```\nThe volume of the cube is \\(\\boxed{216}\\) cubic inches.", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the side length of the cube from which the steel sphere with a 3-inch radius is made. The sphere is formed by removing metal from the corners of the cube, which means the diameter of the sphere is equal to the side length of the cube. Since the radius of the sphere is 3 inches, the diameter of the sphere is \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\) inches. Therefore, the side length of the cube is also 6 inches.\n\nThe volume \\(V\\) of a cube with side length \\(s\\) is given by the formula:\n\\[ V = s^3 \\]\n\nIn this case, the side length \\(s\\) is 6 inches. So, the volume of the cube is:\n\\[ V = 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216 \\text{ cubic inches} \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Side length of the cube\nside_length = 6\n\n# Volume of the cube\nvolume = side_length ** 3\nprint(volume)\n```\n```output\n216\n```\nThe volume of the cube is \\(\\boxed{216}\\) cubic inches.", 'The volume of a sphere is given by the formula $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$, where $r$ is the radius of the sphere. In this problem, the radius of the sphere is 3 inches, so the volume of the sphere is $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi (3)^3 = 36\\pi$ cubic inches. The cube that is used to make the sphere must have a side length of at least 6 inches, since the diameter of the sphere is 6 inches. The volume of a cube is given by the formula $s^3$, where $s$ is the length of a side of the cube. Therefore, the volume of the cube is $6^3 = 216$ cubic inches.', "To solve this problem, we need to determine the side length of the cube that has a steel sphere with a radius of 3 inches inscribed within it. Since the sphere is inscribed in the cube, the diameter of the sphere is equal to the side length of the cube. The diameter of the sphere is twice the radius, so the side length of the cube is \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\) inches.\n\nThe volume \\(V\\) of a cube with side length \\(s\\) is given by the formula \\(V = s^3\\). In this case, the side length \\(s\\) is 6 inches. So, the volume of the cube is \\(6^3 = 216\\) cubic inches.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the radius of the sphere\nradius = 3\n\n# Calculate the side length of the cube\nside_length = 2 * radius\n\n# Calculate the volume of the cube\nvolume_cube = side_length ** 3\nprint(volume_cube)\n```\n```output\n216\n```\nThe volume of the cube is \\(\\boxed{216}\\) cubic inches.", 'The volume of a sphere is given by the formula:\n\\[ V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 \\]\nwhere \\( r \\) is the radius of the sphere. Given that the radius \\( r \\) is 3 inches, we can substitute this value into the formula:\n\\[ V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (3)^3 \\]\nFirst, we calculate \\( 3^3 \\):\n\\[ 3^3 = 27 \\]\nSo the formula becomes:\n\\[ V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\times 27 \\]\nNext, we simplify the expression by multiplying \\( \\frac{4}{3} \\) by 27:\n\\[ V = \\frac{4 \\times 27}{3} \\pi = \\frac{108}{3} \\pi = 36 \\pi \\]\nTherefore, the volume of the sphere is:\n\\[ \\boxed{36\\pi} \\]\n\nThe side length of the cube must be equal to the diameter of the sphere, which is twice the radius. Since the radius of the sphere is 3 inches, the diameter is:\n\\[ 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\text{ inches} \\]\nThe volume of a cube is given by the formula:\n\\[ V = s^3 \\]\nwhere \\( s \\) is the side length of the cube. Substituting \\( s = 6 \\) into the formula, we get:\n\\[ V = 6^3 \\]\nCalculating \\( 6^3 \\):\n\\[ 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216 \\]\nTherefore, the volume of the cube is:\n\\[ \\boxed{216} \\]', "To solve this problem, we need to determine the side length of the cube from which the steel sphere is made. Since the sphere is inscribed within the cube, the diameter of the sphere is equal to the side length of the cube. Given that the radius of the sphere is 3 inches, the diameter of the sphere (which is the side length of the cube) is \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\) inches.\n\nThe volume \\(V\\) of a cube with side length \\(s\\) is given by the formula:\n\\[ V = s^3 \\]\nIn this case, the side length \\(s\\) is 6 inches. So, the volume of the cube is:\n\\[ V = 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216 \\text{ cubic inches} \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given radius of the sphere\nradius = 3\n\n# Calculate the side length of the cube\nside_length = 2 * radius\n\n# Calculate the volume of the cube\nvolume_cube = side_length ** 3\nprint(volume_cube)\n```\n```output\n216\n```\nThe volume of the cube is \\(\\boxed{216}\\) cubic inches.", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the side length of the smallest cube that can contain a sphere with a radius of 3 inches. The diameter of the sphere (which is twice the radius) will be equal to the side length of the cube. Once we have the side length of the cube, we can calculate its volume.\n\n1. The diameter of the sphere is \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\) inches, which is the side length of the cube.\n2. The volume \\(V\\) of a cube with side length \\(s\\) is given by \\(V = s^3\\). In this case, \\(s = 6\\) inches.\n\nSo, the volume of the cube is \\(6^3 = 216\\) cubic inches.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the radius of the sphere\nradius = 3\n\n# Calculate the side length of the cube\nside_length = 2 * radius\n\n# Calculate the volume of the cube\nvolum